var e=JSON.parse(`[[801,"To work with classes on DOM elements, use the methods of \`classList\`. You can add, remove and check classes by means of the methods \`add()\`, \`remove()\` and \`contains()\`.\\nFor example:\\n\\n\`const element = document.getElementById('myElement');\\nelement.classList.add('active');\`\\nThis code adds the class \`active\` to the element.","Working with classes in DOM elements lets you change the styles and the behaviour of elements dynamically. JavaScript provides the \`classList\` object, which includes useful methods for managing classes. Let us look at some of them:\\n\\n- \`add(className)\`: adds the class given.\\n\\n- \`remove(className)\`: removes the class given.\\n\\n- \`toggle(className)\`: adds the class if it is absent, and removes it if it is present.\\n\\n- \`contains(className)\`: checks whether the element contains the class given, returning \`true\` or \`false\`.\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nThese methods make the code cleaner and more understandable, making interaction with CSS classes simpler.","const element = document.getElementById('myElement'); \\n// Adding a class \\nelement.classList.add('active'); \\n// Removing a class \\nelement.classList.remove('active'); \\n// Toggling a class \\nelement.classList.toggle('hidden'); \\n// Checking a class \\nif (element.classList.contains('active')) {\\n  console.log('The element is active'); \\n}"],[802,"The browser begins with a DNS request to obtain the server's IP address, then establishes a TCP connection and sends an HTTP request. Once the HTML document has been received, the parser builds the DOM tree, and in parallel the CSS and the JavaScript are loaded. The CSS forms the CSSOM, then the browser creates the render tree, computes the layout (the geometry of the elements) and carries out the paint (the painting of the pixels).","The general process of rendering\\nWhen a user enters a URL in the address bar, the browser starts a sequence of steps known as the critical rendering path. This process includes several stages: from the resolution of the DNS to the painting of the pixels on the screen.\\nThe main stages\\n\\n- The DNS request: the browser converts the domain name into the server's IP address.\\n- The TCP connection: a connection with the server is established (usually through HTTPS).\\n- The HTTP request: the browser sends a GET request to the server to obtain the HTML document.\\n- The parsing of the HTML: the browser starts to parse the HTML and build the DOM tree. On discovering external resources (CSS, JS) it initiates their loading.\\n- The building of the CSSOM: the CSS rules are converted into the CSS Object Model.\\n- The Render Tree: the combining of the DOM and the CSSOM into a tree containing only the visible elements.\\n- The Layout: the computation of the precise sizes and positions of every element.\\n- The Paint: the painting of the pixels on the screen.\\nAn example with code\\nLet us look at a simple HTML document:\\n\\nThe browser first loads the HTML, then finds the \`<link>\` and starts loading the CSS. The parsing of the HTML continues, but the rendering is blocked until the CSS has fully loaded. Once the CSS has been received, the CSSOM is built, then the render tree. Next the layout and the paint are carried out. The JavaScript at the end is loaded and carried out, which can change the DOM or the CSSOM and cause a recomputation.\\nConclusion\\nAn understanding of the critical rendering path helps you optimise the performance of web pages — by minimising blocking resources and using the asynchronous loading of scripts, for example.","<!DOCTYPE html>\\n<html>\\n<head>\\n  <link rel=\\"stylesheet\\" href=\\"styles.css\\">\\n</head>\\n<body>\\n  <div>Hello World</div>\\n  <script src=\\"app.js\\"><\/script>\\n</body>\\n</html>"],[803,"The main metrics of the loading performance of a page include First Contentful Paint (FCP), Largest Contentful Paint (LCP), First Input Delay (FID), Cumulative Layout Shift (CLS) and Time to Interactive (TTI). FCP measures the time to the first display of content, LCP the loading time of the largest element, FID the delay on the first interaction, CLS visual stability, and TTI the time to full interactivity. These metrics help you assess the user experience and optimise performance.","The metrics of the loading performance of a page\\nThe loading performance of a web page is critically important for the user experience and for ranking in search engines. To assess it, standardised metrics are used, which measure various aspects of the loading: from the first display of content to the stability of the layout. The main metrics are part of the Web Vitals set recommended by Google.\\nThe main metrics\\n\\n- First Contentful Paint (FCP) — the time at which the browser first displays any content (text, an image, a canvas). A good value is less than 1.8 seconds.\\n- Largest Contentful Paint (LCP) — the loading time of the largest visible element (an image, a video, a block of text). The target is less than 2.5 seconds.\\n- First Input Delay (FID) — the delay between the user's first interaction (a click, a key press) and the browser's response. Good is less than 100 ms.\\n- Cumulative Layout Shift (CLS) — a measure of visual stability, assessing unexpected shifts of the layout. A good value is less than 0.1.\\n- Time to Interactive (TTI) — the time at which the page becomes fully interactive (ready for interaction). The target is less than 3.8 seconds.\\nAn example of measurement by means of the Performance API\\n\\nApplication\\nThese metrics are used to monitor the real user experience (RUM) and for laboratory testing (Lighthouse). Optimising for them includes the compression of images, the minimisation of JavaScript, the use of caching and the preloading of critical resources.\\nConclusion: The metrics of the loading performance of a page are necessary for an objective assessment of a site's speed and stability. Measuring and optimising them regularly helps improve the user experience and raise conversion and SEO positions.","// Obtaining the metrics through a PerformanceObserver\\nconst observer = new PerformanceObserver((list) => {\\n  const entries = list.getEntries();\\n  entries.forEach(entry => {\\n    console.log(\`\${entry.name}: \${entry.startTime} ms\`);\\n  });\\n});\\nobserver.observe({ type: 'largest-contentful-paint', buffered: true });\\nobserver.observe({ type: 'first-input', buffered: true });\\nobserver.observe({ type: 'layout-shift', buffered: true });"],[804,"The browser loads the HTML and begins parsing it, building the DOM. On meeting CSS it builds the CSSOM, while JavaScript blocks the parsing until it has been carried out. Then the DOM and the CSSOM are combined into the render tree, after which the layout (the computation of the geometry) and the paint (the painting of the pixels) happen.","The stages of a page's loading\\nWhen a browser receives an HTML document, it passes through several key stages in order to display the page. This process is called the Critical Rendering Path. An understanding of these stages helps developers optimise the speed of loading.\\n1. The parsing of the HTML and the building of the DOM\\nThe browser begins to parse the HTML from the first byte. It converts the markup into the Document Object Model (DOM) — a tree of nodes, where every tag becomes an element. The parsing goes sequentially, but it can be suspended.\\n2. The loading and parsing of the CSS (the CSSOM)\\nWhen the browser meets a \`<link>\` or \`<style>\` tag, it starts loading the CSS. The parsing of the CSS does not block the building of the DOM, but it does block the rendering — the browser will not show the page until it has built the CSSOM (the CSS Object Model). The CSSOM is a tree of rules that are applied to the elements.\\n3. The influence of JavaScript\\nJavaScript can block the parsing of the HTML. If a script is met without the \`async\` or \`defer\` attributes, the browser stops the building of the DOM, loads and carries out the script, and only then continues. That can slow the loading down. The \`defer\` attribute postpones the execution until the parsing has finished, while \`async\` carries the script out asynchronously without blocking the parsing.\\n4. The building of the render tree\\nOnce the DOM and the CSSOM are ready, the browser combines them into the render tree. Only the visible elements get into it (\`<head>\` or elements with \`display: none\` are excluded, for example).\\n5. The Layout (Reflow)\\nAt this stage the browser computes the precise sizes and positions of every element on the page. It is a resource-intensive process, especially with complex layouts.\\n6. The Paint\\nThe final stage is the conversion of the render tree into pixels on the screen. The browser draws the layers and applies the colours, shadows and textures.\\nAn example of code to demonstrate the blocking\\n\\nIn this example the browser will first load the CSS, then stop at the script, carry it out, and only then continue the parsing and the rendering.\\nConclusion\\nAn understanding of the stages of loading helps developers optimise performance: place the CSS in the \`<head>\` and the JavaScript at the end of the \`<body>\` with the \`async\` or \`defer\` attributes, so as not to block the rendering and to improve the user experience.","<!DOCTYPE html>\\n<html>\\n<head>\\n  <link rel=\\"stylesheet\\" href=\\"styles.css\\"> <!-- blocks the rendering -->\\n  <script src=\\"script.js\\"><\/script> <!-- blocks the parsing of the HTML -->\\n</head>\\n<body>\\n  <p>The text will appear after the script has loaded</p>\\n</body>\\n</html>"],[805,"Strings in JavaScript are primitives; they cannot have their own properties. On an attempt to assign a property to a string, JavaScript temporarily wraps it in a String wrapper object, assigns the property, but then the wrapper is destroyed and the property is lost. In strict mode this will raise an error.","Why can strings not hold properties as objects do?\\nIn JavaScript strings belong to the primitive data types. Primitives are immutable values, which have no methods or properties of their own. When you try to access a property of a string or to assign one, JavaScript creates a temporary object wrapper (\`String\`, for example), carries out the operation, and then destroys that wrapper. The property assigned is therefore not preserved.\\nAn example of code\\n\\nHow it works\\n\\n- Primitives (a string, a number, a boolean, null, undefined, a symbol, a bigint) are held by value rather than by reference.\\n- Objects (arrays and functions included) are held by reference and can have dynamic properties.\\n- Autoboxing — the temporary turning of a primitive into an object in order to access methods (\`str.length\`, for example), but that does not preserve changes.\\nConclusion\\nStrings are not intended for holding additional data — for that use objects, a Map or other data structures. An understanding of this difference helps you avoid errors when working with primitives and objects in JavaScript.","let str = 'hello';\\nstr.foo = 'bar';\\nconsole.log(str.foo); // undefined\\n\\n// In strict mode there will be an error\\n'use strict';\\nlet str2 = 'world';\\nstr2.bar = 'baz'; // TypeError: Cannot create property on primitive value"],[806,"In JavaScript objects and arrays are passed by reference, so changes inside a function affect the original. Primitives are passed by value, and changing them does not touch the outer variable. To avoid mutations you can create copies by means of the spread operator or Object.assign.","Passing by reference and by value\\nIn JavaScript the primitive types (numbers, strings, boolean values) are passed by value. That means that when they are passed into a function a copy of the value is created, and changes inside the function do not affect the original variable. Objects and arrays, by contrast, are passed by reference — the function receives a reference to the same object in memory, so mutations inside the function change the original.\\nAn example with an object\\n\\nIn this example the \`person\` object is changed inside the function, since a reference to it is passed.\\nAn example with an array\\n\\nThe \`numbers\` array is also mutated.\\nHow to avoid mutations\\nSo as not to change the original, you can create a copy inside the function. For objects use the spread operator or \`Object.assign\`, for arrays \`slice()\` or the spread:\\n\\nSuch an approach is called immutability and is often applied in React and Redux for the predictability of the state.\\nConclusion\\nAn understanding of passing by reference matters for avoiding unexpected side effects. Use immutable methods when working with state in applications, especially in functional components and the management of state.","function updateUser(user) {\\n  user.name = 'Alice';\\n}\\nconst person = { name: 'Bob' };\\nupdateUser(person);\\nconsole.log(person.name); // 'Alice'\\n\\nfunction addItem(arr) {\\n  arr.push(4);\\n}\\nconst numbers = [1, 2, 3];\\naddItem(numbers);\\nconsole.log(numbers); // [1, 2, 3, 4]\\n\\nfunction safeUpdateUser(user) {\\n  return { ...user, name: 'Alice' };\\n}\\nconst person = { name: 'Bob' };\\nconst updated = safeUpdateUser(person);\\nconsole.log(person.name); // 'Bob'\\nconsole.log(updated.name); // 'Alice'"],[807,"To optimise the sending of analytics on clicks, the techniques of throttling and debouncing are used. Throttling limits the frequency of the sending — to no more than once a second, for example. Debouncing postpones the sending until the user has stopped clicking for a given time. That reduces the number of requests and the load on the server.","The optimisation of the sending of analytics on a click\\nWhen handling users' clicks it matters not to send analytical events on every click, since that can create a superfluous load on the network and the server. For this the techniques of throttling and debouncing are applied, which control the frequency with which functions are called.\\nThrottling\\nThrottling guarantees that a function will be called no more than once in the interval of time stated. With clicks every 100 ms, for example, throttling with an interval of 500 ms will let through only the first click in every 500 ms window.\\n\\nDebouncing\\nDebouncing postpones the call of a function until the interval given has passed since the last call. That is useful when you have to wait for a series of clicks to finish.\\n\\nApplication\\nThrottling suits events that have to be handled regularly better — the sending of analytics on scroll, for example. Debouncing is effective for cases where it matters to wait for a pause — with a search as text is entered, for example.\\nConclusion: use throttling for an even limitation of the frequency, and debouncing for a postponed sending after the user's activity has finished.","function throttle(fn, delay) {\\n  let lastCall = 0;\\n  return function(...args) {\\n    const now = Date.now();\\n    if (now - lastCall >= delay) {\\n      lastCall = now;\\n      fn.apply(this, args);\\n    }\\n  };\\n}\\n\\nconst sendAnalytics = throttle(() => {\\n  console.log('Sending the analytics');\\n}, 1000);\\n\\ndocument.addEventListener('click', sendAnalytics);\\n\\nfunction debounce(fn, delay) {\\n  let timer;\\n  return function(...args) {\\n    clearTimeout(timer);\\n    timer = setTimeout(() => fn.apply(this, args), delay);\\n  };\\n}\\n\\nconst sendAnalytics = debounce(() => {\\n  console.log('Sending the analytics');\\n}, 500);\\n\\ndocument.addEventListener('click', sendAnalytics);"],[808,"Side effects in React are handled by means of the useEffect hook. It lets you carry out code after a component has rendered — requests to an API, subscriptions or timers, for example. It matters to state the dependencies correctly, in order to avoid superfluous calls. To unsubscribe from effects, the cleanup function returned from useEffect is used.","What are side effects in React?\\nSide effects are operations that affect the outer environment or depend on it — HTTP requests, work with localStorage, timers or subscriptions to events, for example. In React such operations cannot be carried out directly during the render, since that can lead to an inconsistent state of the UI. The \`useEffect\` hook is used for this.\\nHow does useEffect work?\\n\`useEffect\` takes two arguments: a function with the effect and an array of dependencies. The function is carried out after every render if the dependencies have changed. If the array is empty, the effect is carried out only once, after the component has mounted. If the dependencies are not stated, the effect is carried out after every render.\\n\\nThe cleanup of effects\\nIf an effect creates a subscription or a timer, it is necessary to return a cleanup function, in order to avoid memory leaks. React will call that function before the repeated execution of the effect or when the component unmounts.\\n\\nThe rules of use\\n\\n- Do not call hooks inside conditions or loops — they have to be at the top level of the component.\\n- State every variable used in the effect in the array of dependencies, in order to avoid bugs with stale data.\\n- For complex logic use several \`useEffect\`s instead of one large one.\\nConclusion: \`useEffect\` is the main tool for working with side effects in React's functional components. The correct management of the dependencies and the cleanup guarantees the stable working of an application and prevents memory leaks.","import { useEffect, useState } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction UserProfile({ userId }) {\\n  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    fetch(\`/api/users/\${userId}\`)\\n      .then(res => res.json())\\n      .then(data => setUser(data));\\n  }, [userId]); // the effect is carried out when userId changes\\n\\n  return <div>{user ? user.name : 'Loading...'}</div>;\\n}\\n\\nuseEffect(() => {\\n  const timer = setInterval(() => {\\n    console.log('Tick');\\n  }, 1000);\\n\\n  return () => clearInterval(timer); // the cleanup\\n}, []);"],[809,"A token is passed in the HTTP Authorization header using the Bearer scheme. The client adds a header of the form Authorization: Bearer to every request requiring authentication. The server extracts the token from that header and checks its validity.","The passing of a token in requests\\nAn authentication token, most often a JWT (JSON Web Token), is passed from the client to the server through the HTTP \`Authorization\` header. That is the standard, secure way, since headers are not cached by the browser and are not saved in the history.\\nThe Bearer scheme\\nThe most widespread scheme is Bearer. The client forms the header as follows:\\n\\nThe server, on receiving the request, extracts the value after the word \\"Bearer\\" and checks the token.\\nAn example in JavaScript (fetch)\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\n\\n- REST APIs and microservices\\n- SPAs (React, Vue, Angular) for authentication\\n- Mobile applications\\nConclusion: Use the Authorization header with the Bearer scheme for the secure, standardised passing of tokens. It is a reliable way, supported by every modern HTTP client and server.","Authorization: Bearer eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9...\\n\\nconst token = 'your_token_here';\\n\\nfetch('https://api.example.com/data', {\\n  method: 'GET',\\n  headers: {\\n    'Authorization': \`Bearer \${token}\`,\\n    'Content-Type': 'application/json'\\n  }\\n})\\n.then(response => response.json())\\n.then(data => console.log(data));"],[810,"In FSD every layer has a strict zone of responsibility: shared contains reusable utilities, entities business entities, features the user's scenarios, widgets compositional blocks, pages the pages, app the global configuration. Layers may import only those below them, which ensures a one-directional direction of the dependencies and makes maintaining the code simpler.","The principle of the organisation of the layers in FSD\\nFeature-Sliced Design (FSD) is a methodology for organising front-end projects, which divides code into layers with clear zones of responsibility. The main idea consists in having every layer responsible for a particular aspect of the application, with the dependencies between them strictly one-directional: from the upper layers to the lower ones. That prevents circular dependencies and makes refactoring simpler.\\nThe layers and their responsibility\\n\\n- app — the global configuration, initialisation, the providers (a Redux store, routing, for example).\\n- pages — the composition of widgets and features for specific pages.\\n- widgets — self-contained blocks of the interface, combining several features (the site's header, for example).\\n- features — the user's scenarios (a login form, a search, for example).\\n- entities — business entities (a user, a product, for example).\\n- shared — reusable utilities, UI components, API clients.\\nAn example of code\\n\\nConclusion\\nFSD helps maintain a clean architecture in large projects, where the separation of responsibility and the predictability of the dependencies matter. This approach is especially useful in teams where scalability and the ease of bringing in changes are required.","// entities/user/model.ts — the user's business logic\\nexport interface User {\\n  id: number;\\n  name: string;\\n}\\n\\n// features/login/ui/LoginForm.tsx — the login scenario\\nimport { User } from 'entities/user';\\n\\nexport const LoginForm = () => {\\n  // uses entities, but not widgets or pages\\n};"],[811,"Function overloading is the ability to define several functions with one name but different parameters (type, quantity or order). The compiler chooses the version needed on the basis of the arguments at the call. It is a variety of static polymorphism, which raises the readability of code. The functions \`print(int)\` and \`print(string)\` can have different implementations, for example.","What is function overloading?\\nFunction overloading is a mechanism that lets you declare several functions with the same name but different parameters. The compiler or interpreter determines which function exactly to call on the basis of the types, the quantity or the order of the arguments. It is one of the forms of static polymorphism, which is resolved at the compilation stage.\\nHow does it work?\\nWhen an overloaded function is called, the compiler analyses the signature of the call (the types and the quantity of the arguments) and matches it against the variants declared. If an exact match is found, the corresponding implementation is called. If there are several matches or none at all, a compilation error arises.\\nAn example in C++\\n\\nWhere is it applied?\\n\\n- In languages with static typing: C++, Java, C#.\\n- To create convenient APIs, where one function handles different types of data.\\n- In the constructors of classes, to initialise objects in different ways.\\nConclusion\\nFunction overloading makes code simpler by making it more intuitive and removing the need to invent different names for similar operations. It is especially useful when working with heterogeneous data, but it requires care in order to avoid ambiguities.","#include <iostream>\\nusing namespace std;\\n\\nvoid print(int i) {\\n    cout << \\"Integer: \\" << i << endl;\\n}\\n\\nvoid print(double f) {\\n    cout << \\"Float: \\" << f << endl;\\n}\\n\\nvoid print(string s) {\\n    cout << \\"String: \\" << s << endl;\\n}\\n\\nint main() {\\n    print(10);      // Integer: 10\\n    print(3.14);    // Float: 3.14\\n    print(\\"Hello\\"); // String: Hello\\n    return 0;\\n}"],[812,"There are several methods for interacting with DOM elements, including \`getElementById()\`, \`getElementsByClassName()\`, \`getElementsByTagName()\` and \`querySelector()\`. For example, \`document.getElementById('myElement')\` returns the element with the identifier given.","Interaction with DOM elements involves the use of various methods for finding and manipulating them. Here are the main methods that are often used:\\n\\n- \`getElementById(id)\`: returns the element with the identifier given.\\n\\n- \`getElementsByClassName(className)\`: returns a collection of every element with the class given. It is a \\"live\\" representation — that is, changes in the DOM are automatically reflected in that collection.\\n\\n- \`getElementsByTagName(tagName)\`: returns a collection of every element with the tag given.\\n\\n- \`querySelector(selector)\`: returns the first element matching the CSS selector given.\\n\\n- \`querySelectorAll(selector)\`: returns every element matching the CSS selector given, in the form of a static NodeList.\\n\\nThese methods let developers easily find and change the elements on a web page, which is the foundation for building interactive, dynamic user interfaces.","const element = document.getElementById('myElement');\\n\\nconst elements = document.getElementsByClassName('myClass');\\n\\nconst elements = document.getElementsByTagName('p');\\n\\nconst element = document.querySelector('.myClass');\\n\\nconst elements = document.querySelectorAll('div.myClass');"],[813,"The public API of a module is the set of functions, classes or variables that the module explicitly exports for use by other parts of an application. Everything not exported is considered private and inaccessible from outside. That lets you hide the internal implementation and provide only the interface necessary. In JavaScript, for example, export is used to declare the public API.","What is the public API of a module?\\nThe public API of a module is the contract the module provides to outside code. It determines which functions, classes, constants or types are available to be imported and used in other modules. The internal details of the implementation, such as helper functions or private variables, stay hidden. It is a key principle of encapsulation, which helps maintain the cleanliness of the code, reduce coupling and make refactoring simpler.\\nHow does it work in JavaScript?\\nIn modern JavaScript (ES6+) the public API of a module is set by means of the \`export\` keyword. You can export individual entities or use \`export default\` for the main export. Everything not exported is private and inaccessible from outside.\\n\\nIn another file you can import only what you need:\\n\\nWhy is it needed?\\nThe public API of a module lets you:\\n\\n- Hide the internal complexity and the details of the implementation.\\n- Reduce the risk of the accidental use of internal functions.\\n- Make the testing and the replacement of modules simpler.\\n- Create clear boundaries between the parts of an application.\\nThat is especially important in large projects, where the modules are developed by different teams or reused in several places.\\nConclusion\\nThe public API of a module is the foundation of modular architecture on the front end. It helps organise code and make it more predictable and convenient to maintain. Use explicit exports in order to control what exactly is available from outside, and avoid exporting everything indiscriminately.","// math.js — a module with a public API\\n\\nexport function add(a, b) {\\n  return a + b;\\n}\\n\\nexport const PI = 3.14159;\\n\\n// A private function, not exported\\nfunction helper() {\\n  console.log('Internal helper');\\n}\\n\\n// app.js\\nimport { add, PI } from './math.js';\\n\\nconsole.log(add(2, 3)); // 5\\nconsole.log(PI); // 3.14159"],[814,"Feature-Sliced Design (FSD) is a methodology for organising front-end architecture, which divides a project into layers (app, pages, widgets, features, entities, shared). The main problems: excessive complexity for small projects, a rigid structure that can hinder fast development, and the risk of creating too large a number of abstractions. FSD also requires strict discipline in the team and can be hard to introduce into an existing project.","The problems when using Feature-Sliced Design (FSD)\\nFeature-Sliced Design (FSD) is an architectural approach for front-end applications, which structures code by functional modules (features) and layers (app, pages, widgets, entities, shared). Despite its advantages in the organisation of large projects, FSD has a number of problems that can arise when it is applied.\\nThe main problems\\n\\n- Excessive complexity for small projects: FSD requires a multitude of layers and modules to be created, which can be superfluous for small applications. That slows development down and increases the time spent on refactoring.\\n- A rigid structure: A strict hierarchy of layers can restrict flexibility. If a simple feature has to be added quickly, for example, the developer has to follow FSD's rules, which can be inconvenient.\\n- The risk of over-engineering: The aspiration to an ideal architecture can lead to the creation of too large a number of abstractions, which makes understanding the code and maintaining it more difficult.\\n- Difficulties of introduction into an existing project: Moving over to FSD in a project that is already working requires substantial efforts of refactoring, which can be expensive and risky.\\n- The team's discipline is required: FSD is effective only if the whole team strictly observes its rules. Otherwise the architecture quickly degrades.\\nAn example of code: the problem of superfluous abstraction\\n\\nIn a small project such an approach can be superfluous, since it would be simpler to write everything in one file.\\nConclusion\\nFSD is worth applying in large projects with long-term maintenance and a large team, where scalability and a clear separation of responsibility matter. For small or prototype projects it is better to use simpler architectural solutions, in order to avoid excessive complexity.","// Instead of a simple component you have to create several files\\n// entities/user/model.ts\\nexport const useUser = () => {\\n  // the logic\\n};\\n\\n// features/login/ui/LoginForm.tsx\\nimport { useUser } from 'entities/user';\\n\\nexport const LoginForm = () => {\\n  const { login } = useUser();\\n  return <button onClick={login}>Login</button>;\\n};\\n\\n// pages/LoginPage.tsx\\nimport { LoginForm } from 'features/login';\\n\\nexport const LoginPage = () => <LoginForm />;"],[815,"A Promise belongs to the microtasks, while setTimeout belongs to the macrotasks. The event loop first carries out every microtask from the queue before moving on to the next macrotask. A Promise is therefore carried out before setTimeout, even if they have the same delay time.","How the Event Loop works\\nJavaScript uses an event loop to manage asynchronous operations. At its basis lies a cycle that sequentially handles the tasks from the queues. There are two types of queue: the microtask queue and the macrotask queue.\\nMicrotasks and macrotasks\\nThe microtasks include Promise.then/catch/finally, MutationObserver and queueMicrotask. The macrotasks include setTimeout, setInterval, I/O operations and DOM events. The event loop first carries out one macrotask, then clears the whole microtask queue before taking the next macrotask.\\nAn example of code\\n\\nIn this example the synchronous code is carried out first (Start, End). Then the event loop checks the microtask queue and carries out the Promise. Only after that does it take the next macrotask — setTimeout.\\nPractical application\\nAn understanding of this mechanism matters for preventing an unexpected order in which code is carried out. If particular code has to be guaranteed to run after every Promise, for example, setTimeout with a zero delay should be used, but remember that it will be carried out after every microtask.\\nConclusion: A Promise is carried out before setTimeout because of the priority of microtasks over macrotasks in the event loop. This knowledge helps you manage asynchronous operations correctly and avoid errors connected with the order in which code is carried out.","console.log('Start');\\n\\nsetTimeout(() => {\\n  console.log('setTimeout');\\n}, 0);\\n\\nPromise.resolve().then(() => {\\n  console.log('Promise');\\n});\\n\\nconsole.log('End');\\n// Output: Start, End, Promise, setTimeout"],[816,"A Map is a key-value collection, where the keys can be of any type. A search by key in a Map is carried out in O(1), which is substantially faster than a search in an array (O(n)). Use a Map when you have to search for elements by a unique identifier often, especially if the keys are not strings.","What is a Map and how does it work?\\nA Map is a built-in data structure in JavaScript, which holds key-value pairs. Unlike ordinary objects, the keys in a Map can be of any type: numbers, strings, objects, functions. The search for an element by key in a Map is carried out in constant time O(1), since it is implemented on the basis of a hash table.\\nAn example of the optimisation of a search\\nImagine you have an array of user objects, and you often have to find a user by their ID. A search in the array requires going through every element (O(n)). Using a Map, you can shorten the search time to O(1).\\n\\nWhen should a Map be applied?\\nA Map is ideally suited to scenarios where the frequent insertion, deletion and search of elements by key is required. When caching data, managing an application's state or working with collections where the keys are not strings, for example. Unlike objects, a Map preserves the order of insertion and has convenient methods such as \`size\`, \`forEach\` and \`has\`.\\nConclusion: Use a Map to optimise a search when the keys can be of any type and fast access to the data is required. That is especially useful in applications with large sets of data or with frequent search operations.","// The array of users\\nconst users = [\\n  { id: 1, name: 'Alice' },\\n  { id: 2, name: 'Bob' },\\n  { id: 3, name: 'Charlie' }\\n];\\n\\n// The search in the array (O(n))\\nfunction findUserById(users, id) {\\n  return users.find(user => user.id === id);\\n}\\n\\n// The creation of a Map for a fast search\\nconst userMap = new Map(users.map(user => [user.id, user]));\\n\\n// The search in the Map (O(1))\\nfunction findUserByIdMap(id) {\\n  return userMap.get(id);\\n}\\n\\nconsole.log(findUserByIdMap(2)); // { id: 2, name: 'Bob' }"],[817,"If a Promise is not resolved (neither resolve nor reject has been called), it stays in the pending state forever. That means the code waiting for it to finish (through then or await) will never be carried out. Such a Promise can lead to a memory leak if references to it remain, since the garbage collector will not delete it while there are active handlers.","What is an unsettled Promise?\\nA Promise in JavaScript represents an asynchronous operation, which can be in one of three states: pending, fulfilled or rejected. If neither resolve nor reject has been called, the Promise stays in the pending state forever. That means the then/catch chain or the await bound to it will never fire.\\nThe practical consequences\\nAn unsettled Promise does not raise an error directly, but it can lead to the following problems:\\n\\n- The code waiting for the result is not carried out, which can break the application's logic.\\n- If there are active references to the Promise (handlers through then, for example), it will not be deleted by the garbage collector, which leads to a memory leak.\\n- In the Node.js environment unsettled Promises can prevent the process from finishing, if they are registered in the event loop.\\nAn example of code\\n\\nHow to avoid it?\\nAlways guarantee that resolve or reject is called in every path of execution of a Promise's executor function. Use error handlers and timeouts to prevent hanging. In modern environments (Node.js, for example) you can use flags of the \`--unhandled-rejections=strict\` kind to track unsettled Promises.\\nConclusion: An unsettled Promise is a source of hidden bugs and memory leaks. Always settle a Promise explicitly, in order to ensure the predictable execution of asynchronous code.","const unresolvedPromise = new Promise((resolve, reject) => {\\n  // Neither resolve nor reject is called\\n  console.log('The Promise has been created but not settled');\\n});\\n\\nunresolvedPromise.then(() => {\\n  console.log('This code will never be carried out');\\n});\\n\\n// After 5 seconds the Promise is still in the pending state\\nsetTimeout(() => console.log(unresolvedPromise), 5000);\\n// Output: Promise { <pending> }"],[818,"Superfluous calls of console.log in a child component arise because of repeated rendering. To avoid them, use React.memo to prevent unnecessary renders if the props have not changed. You can also wrap functions in useCallback and values in useMemo, in order to stabilise the references. That is especially useful when passing callbacks or objects into child components.","Why do superfluous calls of console.log arise?\\nIn React a component is repainted when the state or the props change. If a child component is not optimised, it will render every time the parent updates, even if its own props have not changed. That leads to superfluous calls of console.log that are in the body of the component.\\nHow do you fix it?\\nThe main tool is \`React.memo\`. It wraps a component and prevents a repeated render if the props have stayed the same (a shallow comparison).\\n\\nIn this example \`Child\` will not be repainted when the button is pressed, since the \`value\` prop does not change. If a function or an object is passed, however, they have to be stabilised by means of \`useCallback\` and \`useMemo\`.\\nAdditional advice\\n\\n- Use \`React.StrictMode\` to reveal problems, but remember that it causes a double render in dev mode.\\n- Do not abuse memoisation — apply it only for components that really are repainted often.\\n- Check with React DevTools which components are being repainted.\\nConclusion: Use \`React.memo\` for pure components, and \`useCallback\`/\`useMemo\` to stabilise the props. That reduces the number of unnecessary renders and improves an application's performance.","import React, { memo } from 'react';\\n\\nconst Child = memo(({ value }) => {\\n  console.log('Child rendered');\\n  return <div>{value}</div>;\\n});\\n\\nfunction Parent() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = React.useState(0);\\n  return (\\n    <>\\n      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>Increment</button>\\n      <Child value=\\"static\\" />\\n    </>\\n  );\\n}"],[819,"To reduce the complexity from O(n²) to O(n), you have to replace nested loops with more effective data structures — hash tables, for example. Instead of going through every pair of elements, you can use a single pass with the data written into a dictionary. That lets you find the elements you need in constant time. Such an approach is often applied in tasks of searching for pairs, counting frequencies or checking for duplicates.","What do the complexities O(n²) and O(n) mean?\\nA complexity of O(n²) means that the execution time of an algorithm grows in proportion to the square of the size of the input data. That is typical of nested loops, where for every element of an array a pass over all of the rest is carried out. O(n) is linear complexity, where the number of operations is directly proportional to the size of the data.\\nHow do you move from O(n²) to O(n)?\\nThe main way is to use data structures with fast access, such as hash tables (objects, Map, Set). Instead of searching for a pair for every element by brute force, you can remember the elements already looked at and check the condition in O(1).\\nAn example: the search for two numbers giving a given sum\\n\\nOther techniques\\n\\n- Two pointers — for sorted arrays you can move pointers from different sides, avoiding nested loops.\\n- A sliding window — for tasks with subarrays of a fixed length or with conditions.\\n- Preliminary sorting — sometimes sorting in O(n log n) lets you then apply a linear pass.\\nConclusion\\nThe reduction of the complexity from O(n²) to O(n) is achieved by replacing nested loops with data structures with constant access. That is especially useful when working with large volumes of data, where quadratic algorithms become unacceptably slow. It is always worth analysing whether the full brute force over pairs can be avoided.","// O(n²) — nested loops\\nfunction twoSumSlow(arr, target) {\\n  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {\\n    for (let j = i + 1; j < arr.length; j++) {\\n      if (arr[i] + arr[j] === target) return [i, j];\\n    }\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// O(n) — using a hash table\\nfunction twoSumFast(arr, target) {\\n  const map = new Map();\\n  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {\\n    const complement = target - arr[i];\\n    if (map.has(complement)) return [map.get(complement), i];\\n    map.set(arr[i], i);\\n  }\\n}"],[820,"The isolation of styles prevents CSS conflicts between components. The main approaches: BEM (a methodology for naming classes), CSS Modules (the automatic generation of unique names), the Shadow DOM (encapsulation in web components), Scoped Styles (the scoped attribute in Vue/Svelte) and CSS-in-JS (styles in JavaScript). The choice depends on the framework and on the requirements as regards performance.","What is the isolation of styles?\\nThe isolation of styles is a technique that guarantees that the CSS rules applied to one component do not affect other parts of an application. That is critically important in large projects, where a multitude of developers work on different modules and the accidental overriding of styles can lead to bugs.\\nThe main approaches\\n\\n- BEM (Block Element Modifier) — a methodology for naming classes, \`block__element--modifier\` for example. It creates unique names, reducing the likelihood of conflicts, but it requires discipline from the team.\\n- CSS Modules — a build tool (Webpack, for example), which automatically generates unique class names. An example:\\n\\nAs a result the class turns into \`_button_1a2b3\`.\\n- The Shadow DOM — part of the Web Components specification, creating an isolated DOM tree. The styles inside the Shadow DOM do not penetrate outwards, nor vice versa. An example:\\n\\n- Scoped Styles — used in Vue (through the \`scoped\` attribute) and in Svelte. The compiler adds a unique attribute to the elements and modifies the CSS selectors:\\n\\n- CSS-in-JS — libraries of the Styled Components or Emotion kind generate unique classes on the fly:\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe choice of approach depends on the project: for simple sites BEM will do, for React applications CSS Modules or CSS-in-JS, and for maximum isolation in web components the Shadow DOM. The main thing is to ensure the predictability and the maintainability of the styles.","/* styles.module.css */\\n.button { background: blue; }\\n\\n// Component.jsx\\nimport styles from './styles.module.css';\\n<button className={styles.button}>Click</button>\\n\\nconst host = document.createElement('div');\\nconst shadow = host.attachShadow({mode: 'open'});\\nshadow.innerHTML = \`<style>p { color: red; }</style><p>Hello</p>\`;\\n\\n<style scoped>\\n.button { color: green; }\\n</style>\\n<!-- Turns into -->\\n.button[data-v-abc123] { color: green; }\\n\\nconst Button = styled.button\`\\n  background: blue;\\n\`;"],[821,"Not all scripts block the parsing of HTML. Ordinary scripts without attributes block the parsing until they have fully loaded and been carried out. Scripts with the async or defer attributes do not block the parsing, but they behave differently: async loads in parallel and is carried out immediately after loading, while defer is carried out after the parsing has finished. That lets you speed up the loading of a page.","How scripts affect the parsing of HTML\\nWhen the browser meets a \`<script>\` tag without attributes, it stops the parsing of the HTML, loads the script, carries it out, and only then continues parsing the document. That can slow the display of the page down, especially if the script is heavy or loads from a slow server.\\nThe async and defer attributes\\nThe \`async\` attribute lets the script be loaded in parallel with the parsing of the HTML. As soon as the script has loaded, it is carried out, suspending the parsing. The \`defer\` attribute also loads the script in parallel, but it postpones its execution until the parsing of the document has fully finished. Neither attribute blocks the parsing, but \`defer\` guarantees the order in which the scripts are carried out, whereas \`async\` does not.\\nAn example of code\\n\\nConclusion\\nUse \`defer\` for scripts that have to be carried out after the DOM has fully loaded, and \`async\` for independent scripts — analytics, for example. That improves performance and the user experience.","<!-- A blocking script -->\\n<script src=\\"script.js\\"><\/script>\\n\\n<!-- Non-blocking async -->\\n<script src=\\"script.js\\" async><\/script>\\n\\n<!-- Non-blocking defer -->\\n<script src=\\"script.js\\" defer><\/script>"],[822,"Time To First Byte (TTFB) is the time from the sending of a request to the receipt of the first byte of the response from the server. It includes the delays of the network, the handling of the request by the server and the beginning of the transfer of the data. A low TTFB points to a fast server and a good network connection. A high TTFB can be caused by a slow server, a poor network or unoptimised code.","What is TTFB?\\nTime To First Byte (TTFB) is a metric measuring the time between the sending of an HTTP request by the client and the moment the client receives the first byte of the response from the server. It reflects the delay of the network and the speed with which the request is handled on the server. TTFB is an important indicator of the performance of web applications, since it affects the user's perception of the speed of a page's loading.\\nWhat does TTFB consist of?\\nTTFB includes several stages:\\n\\n- The delay of the network (the time it takes to transfer the request from the client to the server).\\n- The time it takes the server to handle the request (including the execution of the code, requests to the database and so on).\\n- The time at which the transfer of the response begins (the first byte).\\nThe lower the TTFB, the faster the user starts to receive content. The optimal value of TTFB is less than 200 ms, a good one is up to 500 ms, and a bad one is more than 1 second.\\nAn example of measuring TTFB\\nIn the browser TTFB can be measured by means of the developer tools (the Network tab) or through JavaScript:\\n\\nHow do you improve TTFB?\\nTo reduce TTFB you can:\\n\\n- Optimise the server code (reduce the execution time of the queries).\\n- Use caching (a CDN, a database cache).\\n- Choose faster hosting or a geographically closer server.\\n- Minimise the size of the response (compression, the removal of superfluous data).\\nConclusion: TTFB is a key metric for assessing the speed of a server and a network. Optimising it improves the user experience and SEO, especially for sites with dynamic content.","const startTime = performance.now();\\nfetch('https://example.com')\\n  .then(response => {\\n    const ttfb = performance.now() - startTime;\\n    console.log('TTFB:', ttfb, 'ms');\\n  });"],[823,"- \`innerHTML\` is used to obtain or set the HTML content of an element, including any nested tags. By means of \`innerHTML\`, for example, you can insert other elements such as \`<strong>\` inside the text.\\n\\n- \`innerText\` works only with text and ignores any HTML tags, returning only the visible text.\\nFor example, if an element contains \`<strong>Text</strong>\`, \`innerText\` will return 'Text', while \`innerHTML\` will return '<strong>Text</strong>'.","The difference between \`innerHTML\` and \`innerText\` consists in how they work with the content of HTML elements.\\n\\n- \`innerHTML\`: this property lets you obtain or set the whole HTML content of an element, including any nested tags. If you have the element \`<div id=\\"myDiv\\"><strong>Hello</strong></div>\`, for example, and you use \`document.getElementById('myDiv').innerHTML\`, that will return \`<strong>Hello</strong>\`. You can also insert HTML, for example:\\n\\nThat will create a new paragraph inside \`myDiv\`.\\n\\n- \`innerText\`: this property returns only the textual content of an element, ignoring any HTML tags. For the same element, if you call \`document.getElementById('myDiv').innerText\`, that will return 'Hello'. That is useful when you have to obtain only the visible text on a page, without taking the structure of the HTML into account. For example:\\n\\nWhen choosing between \`innerHTML\` and \`innerText\`, it thus matters to understand whether you have to work with HTML content or simply to obtain text.","document.getElementById('myDiv').innerHTML = '<p>New text</p>';\\n\\nconst text = document.getElementById('myDiv').innerText; \\nconsole.log(text); // 'Hello'"],[824,"First Contentful Paint (FCP) is a performance metric that measures the time from the beginning of a page's loading to the moment the browser displays the first fragment of content — text or an image, for example. It helps you assess how quickly the user sees that the page is starting to load. A good value of FCP is less than 1.8 seconds.","What is First Contentful Paint (FCP)?\\nFirst Contentful Paint (FCP) is one of the key performance metrics of web pages, forming part of the Core Web Vitals. It measures the time in milliseconds from the moment the user starts to move to the page (enters the URL or presses a link, for example) to the moment the browser first paints any textual or graphical content. That can be text, an image, an SVG, a canvas or even a non-empty \`<div>\` element. FCP does not take into account background images or fonts loaded through \`@font-face\`.\\nHow is FCP measured?\\nFCP is fixed by the browser at the moment the first painting of content in the viewport happens. To measure it, tools of the Lighthouse, PageSpeed Insights kind or the Performance API in the browser are used. An example of obtaining FCP through JavaScript:\\n\\nWhy does FCP matter?\\nFCP directly affects the user's perception of the speed of loading. If the FCP is high (more than 2.5 seconds), the user may think the page is not working and leave. A low FCP (less than 1.8 seconds) creates the feeling of fast loading. The metric is especially critical for content sites, online shops and news portals, where the first impression decides a great deal.\\nHow do you improve FCP?\\n\\n- Minimise the render-blocking CSS and JavaScript.\\n- Use critical CSS (inline styles for the first screen).\\n- Optimise the images (compression, the modern WebP formats).\\n- Set up the caching of resources through a CDN.\\n- Remove unused code and libraries.\\nConclusion\\nFCP is a basic metric for assessing the initial loading of a page. Improving it directly raises the user experience and can have a positive effect on SEO and conversion. It is recommended to monitor FCP in real conditions regularly and to optimise the critical rendering paths.","new PerformanceObserver((list) => {\\n  const entries = list.getEntries();\\n  entries.forEach(entry => {\\n    console.log('FCP:', entry.startTime, 'ms');\\n  });\\n}).observe({ type: 'paint', buffered: true });"],[825,"Yes, accessing the DOM through JavaScript can cause a reflow. Reflow is the process of recomputing the geometry of the elements on a page. When you read or change properties affecting the sizes or the positions of elements (offsetHeight, clientWidth, scrollTop, for example), the browser can carry out a forced reflow in order to return the current values. That can have a negative effect on performance, especially with frequent operations.","What is reflow and how is it connected with the DOM?\\nReflow (or layout) is the stage of a page's rendering at which the browser computes the sizes and positions of every element. It happens on the initial loading of the page, and also on any changes that can affect the geometry: a change of the window's size, the addition/removal of elements, a change of styles (width, height, margin, padding and so on).\\nJavaScript can both read and change the DOM. Some read operations, such as obtaining offsetHeight, clientWidth, scrollTop or getComputedStyle, can force the browser to carry out a reflow in order to return the current values. That is called a forced synchronous reflow.\\nExamples of operations causing a reflow\\n\\n- The reading of properties: offsetHeight, offsetWidth, clientHeight, clientWidth, scrollTop, scrollLeft, getBoundingClientRect, getComputedStyle.\\n- A change of styles affecting the geometry: width, height, margin, padding, display, position, float and so on.\\n- The addition or removal of elements from the DOM.\\n- A change of the content (textContent, for example), if it affects the sizes.\\nA practical example\\n\\nIn this example every access to offsetHeight and every change of width can cause a reflow. To minimise the number of reflows, it is recommended to group the read and write operations, or to use techniques such as reading every value before the changes.\\nHow to avoid superfluous reflows\\n\\n- Use classes to change styles instead of changing the properties directly.\\n- Work with elements out of the flow (with display: none, for example, then change them, then show them).\\n- Use a documentFragment for the mass addition of elements.\\n- Cache the values that can cause a reflow, if they do not change.\\nConclusion: An understanding of reflow matters for optimising the performance of web pages. Avoid frequent forced reflows, especially in animations or when working with large lists. Group the operations and use modern approaches such as requestAnimationFrame to synchronise the changes with the browser's rendering cycle.","const el = document.getElementById('myElement');\\n// Reading offsetHeight causes a reflow\\nconst height = el.offsetHeight;\\n// Changing the width also causes a reflow\\nel.style.width = '200px';\\n// If you read after the change, there will be another reflow\\nconst newHeight = el.offsetHeight;"],[826,"When the browser meets CSS (external or internal), it continues parsing the HTML but suspends the rendering until the CSSOM has fully loaded and been built. On meeting ordinary JavaScript (without async/defer), the browser completely stops the parsing of the HTML, loads and carries out the script, and only then continues. That is done because the script can change the DOM or the CSSOM.","The influence of CSS and JavaScript on the parsing of HTML\\nThe browser builds the DOM (Document Object Model) from the HTML and the CSSOM (CSS Object Model) from the CSS. These two trees are combined into the Render Tree for the painting of the page. CSS and JavaScript affect this process differently.\\nCSS (render blocking)\\nWhen the HTML parser meets a \`<link rel=\\"stylesheet\\">\` or \`<style>\` tag, it does not stop the parsing of the HTML, but it does block the rendering. The browser will not display the page until the CSSOM has been fully built. That is necessary in order to avoid FOUC (a Flash of Unstyled Content).\\nJavaScript (parser blocking)\\nWhen the parser meets an ordinary \`<script>\` tag (without the \`async\` or \`defer\` attributes), it completely stops the parsing of the HTML. The browser loads the script (if it is external), carries it out, and only then continues the parsing. That is connected with the fact that a script can change the DOM (through \`document.write\`) or access the CSSOM.\\nAn example\\n\\nIn this example the browser will first load the CSS, then stop at the script, load and carry it out, and only then continue the parsing and the painting.\\nOptimisation\\n\\n- For CSS: use \`media\` attributes in order to state that the styles are not critical for the initial rendering.\\n- For JavaScript: use \`async\` (the script is carried out immediately after loading, without blocking the parsing) or \`defer\` (the script is carried out after the parsing of the HTML has finished).\\nConclusion: An understanding of render blocking and parser blocking is critically important for optimising a page's loading speed (the Core Web Vitals). Use \`async\`/\`defer\` for scripts and minimise the critical CSS for a fast first display.","<!DOCTYPE html>\\n<html>\\n<head>\\n  <link rel=\\"stylesheet\\" href=\\"styles.css\\"> <!-- Render blocking -->\\n  <script src=\\"script.js\\"><\/script> <!-- Parser blocking -->\\n</head>\\n<body>\\n  <p>Hello</p>\\n</body>\\n</html>"],[827,"The Provider is a component from React's Context API, which passes the value of the context to every child component. It wraps part of the tree of components and gives them access to the data without an explicit passing through props. That makes managing global state simpler — the theme or authorisation, for example.","The purpose of the Provider in the Context API\\nThe Provider is a wrapper component, which makes the value of a context available to every child component in the React tree. It solves the problem of «prop drilling», where data has to be passed through a multitude of intermediate components.\\nHow it works\\nThe Provider takes a \`value\` prop, which becomes available through the \`useContext\` hook or a Consumer. When the value changes, every subscribed component is repainted.\\nAn example of code\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe Provider is used for the centralised management of state in React applications, when the data is needed by many components at different levels of nesting. That is especially useful for themes, localisation or a user's data.","import React, { createContext, useContext, useState } from 'react';\\n\\nconst ThemeContext = createContext();\\n\\nexport function ThemeProvider({ children }) {\\n  const [theme, setTheme] = useState('light');\\n  return (\\n    <ThemeContext.Provider value={{ theme, setTheme }}>\\n      {children}\\n    </ThemeContext.Provider>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nfunction ChildComponent() {\\n  const { theme, setTheme } = useContext(ThemeContext);\\n  return (\\n    <button onClick={() => setTheme(theme === 'light' ? 'dark' : 'light')}>\\n      Current theme: {theme}\\n    </button>\\n  );\\n}"],[828,"In JavaScript the + operator, when adding a string and a number, brings the number to a string. If one of the operands is a string, the other is also converted into a string. The exception: if both operands are numbers, arithmetic addition is carried out. Objects are also converted through toString or valueOf.","Implicit type coercion in JavaScript\\nThe \`+\` operator in JavaScript behaves differently depending on the types of the operands. If at least one operand is a string, the second is converted into a string and concatenation is carried out. If both operands are numbers, arithmetic addition happens. Objects are first converted into primitives through the \`valueOf\` or \`toString\` methods.\\nExamples\\n\\nPractical application\\nAn understanding of type coercion helps you avoid unexpected errors in code, especially when working with user input or an API. It is recommended to use explicit conversion (\`String()\`, \`Number()\`) in order to raise readability and predictability.\\nConclusion: A knowledge of the rules of implicit type coercion is necessary for debugging and writing reliable code in JavaScript, especially in the conditions of dynamic typing.","console.log(1 + '2'); // '12' (the number 1 is converted into a string)\\nconsole.log('3' + 4 + 5); // '345' (left to right: '3'+4='34', then '34'+5='345')\\nconsole.log(1 + 2 + '3'); // '33' (first 1+2=3, then 3+'3'='33')\\nconsole.log(true + false); // 1 (true=1, false=0)\\nconsole.log([] + {}); // '[object Object]' (an empty array -> '', an object -> '[object Object]')"],[829,"The subscription to changes in the Context API happens through a consumer component. A component using useContext or Consumer subscribes to the context automatically. When the value passed into the Provider changes, every subscribed component is re-rendered. That lets you pass data deep down the tree without prop drilling.","How the subscription to changes in the Context API works\\nIn React's Context API the subscription to changes is implemented automatically when the \`useContext\` hook or the \`Consumer\` component is used. When a component calls \`useContext(MyContext)\`, it registers as a subscriber to that context. When the value passed into the \`Provider\` changes, React notifies every subscribed component and starts their repeated render.\\nAn example of code\\n\\nThe peculiarities of the subscription\\n\\n- The subscription happens at the level of the context rather than to individual fields of the value. If the value is an object, any change of the object will cause a repaint of every subscriber.\\n- For optimisation you can memoise the value by means of \`useMemo\`, in order to avoid superfluous renders.\\n- Components not using the context do not subscribe and are not repainted when it changes.\\nConclusion\\nThe Context API is convenient for passing global data (the theme, authorisation, the language) without prop drilling. It is worth applying it with care for frequently changing data, however, since that can cause excessive renders. For complex state it is better to use Redux or other state-management libraries.","import React, { createContext, useContext, useState } from 'react';\\n\\nconst ThemeContext = createContext('light');\\n\\nfunction App() {\\n  const [theme, setTheme] = useState('light');\\n  return (\\n    <ThemeContext.Provider value={{ theme, setTheme }}>\\n      <Toolbar />\\n    </ThemeContext.Provider>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nfunction Toolbar() {\\n  const { theme, setTheme } = useContext(ThemeContext);\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <p>Current theme: {theme}</p>\\n      <button onClick={() => setTheme(theme === 'light' ? 'dark' : 'light')}>\\n        Toggle\\n      </button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}"],[830,"The getItem method returns null if the key does not exist in localStorage. If that value is not checked, the code can crash with an error on an attempt to call methods on null — JSON.parse(null), for example. Always check the result of getItem for null before using it.","Why null matters when working with localStorage\\nThe \`localStorage.getItem(key)\` method returns \`null\` if the key given is absent from the store. That is the standard behaviour, which has to be taken into account in order to avoid runtime errors.\\nAn example of the problem\\nLet us look at typical code without a check:\\n\\nIf the key 'user' is absent, \`getItem\` will return \`null\`, and \`JSON.parse(null)\` will raise an exception.\\nThe correct approach\\nAlways check the result before using it:\\n\\nYou can also use the nullish coalescing operator (\`??\`) to set a default value:\\n\\nConclusion\\nA check for null when obtaining data from localStorage is mandatory for the stable working of an application, especially on the first run or after the store has been cleared.","const data = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem('user'));\\nconsole.log(data.name); // An error if the key is absent\\n\\nconst raw = localStorage.getItem('user');\\nif (raw === null) {\\n  console.log('The data has not been found');\\n} else {\\n  const data = JSON.parse(raw);\\n  console.log(data.name);\\n}\\n\\nconst data = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem('user') ?? '{}');"],[831,"The loading state should be set to true before the asynchronous operation begins, before the try block. In the finally block or after try-catch it has to be reset to false. That guarantees that loading will be active while the request is being carried out and will be switched off correctly even if an error arises.","The correct placement of the loading state\\nThe loading state should be managed outside the try and catch blocks, in order to ensure the predictable behaviour of the interface. Setting loading to true happens immediately before the call of the asynchronous function, and the reset after the operation has finished, regardless of the result.\\nAn example of the implementation\\n\\nWhy this matters\\n\\n- If you set loading inside try, then on an error before loading is set it will stay false, and the user will not see the loading indicator.\\n- If you reset loading only in catch, then on a successful execution it will stay true forever.\\n- The finally block guarantees that the code will be carried out in any case, which makes it the ideal place for resetting loading.\\nConclusion\\nSet loading to true before try and reset it in finally (or after try-catch). That ensures the correct display of the loading state in every scenario: success, an error or a premature termination.","async function fetchData() {\\n  setLoading(true); // We set it before try\\n  try {\\n    const response = await api.get('/data');\\n    setData(response.data);\\n  } catch (error) {\\n    setError(error.message);\\n  } finally {\\n    setLoading(false); // We reset it in finally\\n  }\\n}"],[832,"A custom hook is worth creating when one and the same logic repeats in several components. That improves readability, testability and the reuse of code. A hook is also justified if the logic is complex and you want to isolate it from the UI. Work with an API, subscriptions, localStorage or forms, for example.","When should logic be moved out into a custom hook?\\nCustom hooks are a way of extracting repeating logic from components. They let you separate state and side effects from the presentation, following the principle of the separation of responsibility. The main signal for creating a hook is the duplication of code: if you see identical logic in two or more components, that is a candidate for being moved out.\\nExamples of use\\n\\n- Work with an API (the loading of data, the handling of errors, loading)\\n- The management of forms (validation, submission)\\n- Subscriptions to events (resize, scroll, WebSocket)\\n- Work with localStorage or cookies\\n- Timers and intervals\\nAn example of code\\n\\nConclusion\\nCustom hooks are worth applying when logic with state or effects has to be reused between components. They make code cleaner and make testing and maintenance simpler, especially in large projects.","import { useState, useEffect } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction useWindowWidth() {\\n  const [width, setWidth] = useState(window.innerWidth);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    const handleResize = () => setWidth(window.innerWidth);\\n    window.addEventListener('resize', handleResize);\\n    return () => window.removeEventListener('resize', handleResize);\\n  }, []);\\n\\n  return width;\\n}\\n\\n// The use in a component\\nfunction MyComponent() {\\n  const width = useWindowWidth();\\n  return <div>Window width: {width}</div>;\\n}"],[833,"The finally block guarantees that code will be carried out after an asynchronous operation has finished, regardless of whether it finished successfully or with an error. That matters for freeing resources, closing connections or hiding loading indicators. Unlike then and catch, finally is always carried out, even if an exception arose in them.","What is finally needed for in asynchronous code?\\nWhen working with asynchronous operations such as requests to a server or the reading of files, code often has to be carried out after the operation has finished, regardless of its result. For example, a loading indicator has to be hidden, the connection with a database closed or memory freed. The finally block in promises and in the async/await construction guarantees that this code will be carried out in any case.\\nHow does it work?\\nThe finally method returns a new promise, which resolves after the original promise has been settled, but before any then or catch handler is carried out. If an exception arises in finally, it will be passed further along the chain. That makes finally the ideal place for cleaning up resources.\\nAn example of code\\n\\nConclusion\\nUse finally for the guaranteed execution of cleanup code, such as the closing of connections, the freeing of locks or the hiding of UI elements, in order to avoid leaks of resources and an incorrect state of the application.","async function fetchData() {\\n  showLoader();\\n  try {\\n    const response = await fetch('/api/data');\\n    const data = await response.json();\\n    return data;\\n  } catch (error) {\\n    console.error('Error:', error);\\n    throw error;\\n  } finally {\\n    hideLoader(); // Will always be carried out\\n  }\\n}"],[834,"An event in the context of the DOM is an action happening on a web page — a mouse click or a key press, for example. An event handler is a function that is carried out when that event happens. To handle an event you can use the \`addEventListener()\` method, for example:\\n\\n\`document.getElementById('myButton').addEventListener('click', function() {\\nalert('The button has been pressed!');\\n});\`\\nThis code calls the function when the button is pressed.","Events in the context of the DOM are actions that happen on a web page and that the browser can track. That can be any action the user carries out — a mouse click, the movement of the mouse, key presses, a change of an input field and so on. When an event happens, it creates an event object, which contains information about what happened.\\nTo handle events you can use the \`addEventListener()\` method, which lets you bind an event handler to a particular element. An example of handling a click event on a button:\\n\\nIn this example, when the user presses the button with the identifier \`myButton\`, a function fires that shows a message.\\nYou can also handle events by means of the built-in attributes, \`onclick\` for example, but the use of \`addEventListener()\` is preferable, since it lets you bind several handlers to one event and manage events more flexibly. Here is an example using \`onclick\`:\\n\\nIt matters to note that events can bubble — that is, they can \\"rise\\" up the DOM hierarchy from a child element to the parent, which lets you use event delegation to handle events on parent elements.","document.getElementById('myButton').addEventListener('click', function() {\\n  alert('The button has been pressed!'); \\n});\\n\\ndocument.getElementById('myButton').onclick = function() {\\n  alert('The button has been pressed!'); \\n};"],[835,"A component needs optimisation if it is repainted often without its input data changing, causes delays in the interface or consumes a lot of resources. Use the React DevTools Profiler to reveal the bottlenecks. Pay attention to superfluous re-renders of child components when the props are unchanged.","The signs that optimisation is needed\\nThe main signal is a noticeable slowing of the interface during the user's interaction. A component may be repainted too often, even when its props and state do not change. That is especially critical for large lists, complex forms or animations.\\nThe tools of diagnosis\\n\\n- The React DevTools Profiler — it records and shows which components are repainted and how long that takes.\\n- The Performance tab in the browser — it helps assess the overall load on the main thread.\\n- Logging by means of \`console.log\` in the body of the component — a simple way of seeing the frequency of the calls.\\nA practical example\\n\\nIn this example \`ExpensiveList\` will be repainted on every click, although \`items\` does not change. The use of \`React.memo\` will prevent the superfluous re-renders.\\nConclusion\\nOptimisation is justified when profiling shows real problems of performance. It is not worth optimising in advance — first measure, then apply \`React.memo\`, \`useMemo\` or \`useCallback\` only for the bottlenecks.","function ExpensiveList({ items }) {\\n  console.log('ExpensiveList rendered');\\n  return items.map(item => <div key={item.id}>{item.name}</div>);\\n}\\n\\nfunction App() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n  const items = useMemo(() => [{ id: 1, name: 'A' }], []);\\n  return (\\n    <>\\n      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>Click</button>\\n      <ExpensiveList items={items} />\\n    </>\\n  );\\n}"],[836,"If you do not state the array of dependencies in useEffect, the effect will be carried out after every render of the component. That can lead to an endless loop if the state is changed inside the effect. To have it carried out only on mounting you have to pass an empty array []. Stating the dependencies is an important practice for controlling side effects.","What happens without an array of dependencies?\\nWhen you use the \`useEffect\` hook without the second argument (the array of dependencies), the effect will be started after every render of the component. That means that on any change of the state or the props that causes a repeated render, the effect will be carried out again.\\nWhy is that a problem?\\nIf inside such an effect you change the state (through \`setState\`, for example), that will cause a new render, which will start the effect again, and so on endlessly. That leads to the browser hanging or to errors.\\nAn example of code\\n\\nIn this example every render increases \`count\`, which causes a new render and starts the effect again.\\nHow do you state the dependencies correctly?\\n\\n- An empty array \`[]\` — the effect is carried out only on mounting and unmounting.\\n- An array with variables \`[count]\` — the effect is carried out only when those variables change.\\n- Without an array — the effect is carried out after every render (use with care).\\nConclusion\\nAlways state the array of dependencies in \`useEffect\`, in order to control when the side effect should be carried out. That prevents unexpected behaviour and improves an application's performance.","import React, { useState, useEffect } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction Counter() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n\\n  // Without dependencies — an endless loop\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    setCount(count + 1);\\n  });\\n\\n  return <div>{count}</div>;\\n}"],[837,"localStorage provides methods for holding data in the browser. The main methods: setItem(key, value) to save, getItem(key) to obtain, removeItem(key) to delete a single record, clear() to clear the whole store, and also key(index) for access by index and the length property for obtaining the number of records.","The main methods of localStorage\\nlocalStorage is part of the Web Storage API, which lets you hold data in the browser with no period of validity. The data is saved even after the tab or the browser has been closed. All of the methods are synchronous and work only with strings.\\nThe methods and their use\\n\\n- setItem(key, value) — saves a value at a key. If the key already exists, the value is overwritten.\\n- getItem(key) — returns the value at a key, or null if the key has not been found.\\n- removeItem(key) — deletes the record at a key.\\n- clear() — deletes every record from localStorage.\\n- key(index) — returns the name of the key at an index (starting from 0).\\n- length — a property showing the number of elements saved.\\nAn example of code\\n\\nImportant peculiarities\\n\\n- Every value is automatically converted into a string. For objects use JSON.stringify() and JSON.parse().\\n- The store is limited in size (usually 5-10 MB per domain).\\n- The data is available only within one protocol and domain.\\nConclusion: localStorage is convenient for holding the user's settings, tokens or the caching of data that has to be preserved between sessions. It is not worth holding confidential information, however, since the data is accessible through JavaScript.","// We save the data\\nlocalStorage.setItem('username', 'John');\\nlocalStorage.setItem('theme', 'dark');\\n\\n// We obtain the data\\nconst user = localStorage.getItem('username'); // 'John'\\n\\n// We delete one record\\nlocalStorage.removeItem('theme');\\n\\n// We obtain the number of records\\nconsole.log(localStorage.length); // 1\\n\\n// Access by index\\nconst firstKey = localStorage.key(0); // 'username'\\n\\n// We clear the whole store\\nlocalStorage.clear();"],[838,"localStorage lets you hold data in the browser with no period of validity. To obtain data the getItem() method is used, which takes a key and returns a string. If the key has not been found, null is returned. The data is held in the form of strings, so objects have to be serialised through JSON.stringify() and parsed through JSON.parse().","What is localStorage and how do you work with it?\\nlocalStorage is part of the Web Storage API, providing the ability to hold data in the user's browser. The data is preserved between sessions and has no period of validity until it is deleted by hand or through JavaScript. Unlike cookies, localStorage is not sent to the server with every HTTP request, which makes it effective for holding settings, tokens or cached data.\\nThe getItem() method\\nTo obtain data from localStorage the \`getItem(key)\` method is used, where key is a string corresponding to the key under which the data was saved. The method returns a string, or null if the key is absent. Since localStorage holds only strings, to work with objects or arrays it is necessary to use JSON.parse().\\n\\nPractical application\\nlocalStorage is often used to hold the user's settings (the theme, the language), the state of the interface (expanded menus) or temporary data that requires no server handling. You can save the filter chosen on a page, for example, so that it is restored on a reload.\\nConclusion\\nlocalStorage is a simple, fast way of holding data on the client, suitable for small volumes of information (up to 5-10 MB). Use it to cache settings or state, but avoid holding sensitive data, since it is accessible through JavaScript.","// The saving of an object\\nconst user = { name: 'Alice', age: 30 };\\nlocalStorage.setItem('user', JSON.stringify(user));\\n\\n// The obtaining and parsing\\nconst storedUser = localStorage.getItem('user');\\nif (storedUser) {\\n  const parsedUser = JSON.parse(storedUser);\\n  console.log(parsedUser.name); // Alice\\n} else {\\n  console.log('The key has not been found');\\n}"],[839,"try/catch lets you intercept and handle the errors arising during the execution of asynchronous operations such as requests to a server. Without it, unhandled exceptions can lead to the application crashing or to unpredictable behaviour. It is a key mechanism for building stable, fault-tolerant code.","What is try/catch needed for in asynchronous requests?\\nAsynchronous operations such as HTTP requests, the reading of files or work with databases can finish with an error for a multitude of reasons: network failures, incorrect data, timeouts or problems on the server. Without the handling of errors such failures will stay unnoticed and can lead to the application crashing or to an incorrect state of the data.\\nHow does it work?\\nThe try/catch construction lets you wrap asynchronous code and intercept any exceptions that may arise during its execution. In the try block the potentially dangerous code is carried out, while in the catch block the error is handled. That gives you the ability to finish the operation gracefully, show the user an understandable message or carry out alternative actions.\\nAn example of code\\n\\nWhere is it applied?\\n\\n- Web applications: the handling of errors of API requests\\n- Server applications: work with databases and external services\\n- Mobile applications: network calls and the loading of data\\nConclusion\\nThe use of try/catch with asynchronous requests is a mandatory practice for building reliable applications. It prevents unhandled exceptions, improves the user experience and makes debugging simpler. Always wrap asynchronous operations in try/catch, especially when working with external resources.","async function fetchUserData(userId) {\\n  try {\\n    const response = await fetch(\`/api/users/\${userId}\`);\\n    if (!response.ok) {\\n      throw new Error(\`HTTP error! status: \${response.status}\`);\\n    }\\n    const data = await response.json();\\n    return data;\\n  } catch (error) {\\n    console.error('An error while loading the user data:', error);\\n    // You can show a notification to the user\\n    throw error; // We throw it further, if needed\\n  }\\n}"],[840,"If you pass a new function into a memoised component, React.memo will not be able to prevent the re-render, since on every render of the parent a new reference to the function is created. The comparison of props happens by reference, and a new function is considered a change. To solve it, useCallback is used, which stabilises the reference to the function between renders.","How React.memo works\\nReact.memo is a higher-order component, which remembers the result of a render and skips a repeated render if the props have not changed. The comparison of the props is carried out shallowly, by references. If you pass a new function, its reference will differ from the previous one, and React.memo will consider that the props have changed, which will lead to a re-render.\\nAn example of the problem\\n\\nEvery time setCount is called the parent re-renders, a new arrow function is created for onClick, and Child re-renders too, despite React.memo.\\nThe solution with useCallback\\n\\nuseCallback returns one and the same reference to the function until the dependencies change (in this case an empty array). Now React.memo sees that onClick has not changed and skips the re-render of Child.\\nConclusion\\nPassing a new function into a memoised component nullifies the benefit of the memoisation. Use useCallback to stabilise the references to the functions passed as props, especially if the component is heavy or re-renders often.","const Child = React.memo(({ onClick }) => {\\n  console.log('Child rendered');\\n  return <button onClick={onClick}>Click</button>;\\n});\\n\\nfunction Parent() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n  return (\\n    <>\\n      <p>{count}</p>\\n      <Child onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)} />\\n    </>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nfunction Parent() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n  const handleClick = useCallback(() => {\\n    setCount(c => c + 1);\\n  }, []);\\n  return (\\n    <>\\n      <p>{count}</p>\\n      <Child onClick={handleClick} />\\n    </>\\n  );\\n}"],[841,"localStorage can hold only strings. If you try to save an object, an array or a number, they will be automatically converted into a string through the toString() method, which will lead to the loss of data or to an incorrect format. Before saving, the data therefore has to be serialised into JSON by means of JSON.stringify(), and deserialised through JSON.parse() when read.","Why does localStorage require serialisation?\\nlocalStorage is a mechanism for holding data in the browser, which works exclusively with strings. When you try to save a value that is not a string (an object, an array, a number or a boolean value, for example), the browser automatically calls the \`toString()\` method for that value. For objects that leads to the string \`[object Object]\` being saved, which makes the data useless.\\nHow do you work with data correctly?\\nFor the correct saving and restoration of complex data structures it is necessary to use JSON serialisation. The \`JSON.stringify()\` method converts an object or an array into a JSON string, while \`JSON.parse()\` restores the original structure.\\n\\nExamples of problems without serialisation\\n\\n- Saving a number: \`localStorage.setItem('count', 42)\` — the string '42' will be saved, but when read it will be a string rather than a number.\\n- Saving an array: \`localStorage.setItem('arr', [1,2,3])\` — the string '1,2,3' will be saved, which is not correct JSON.\\n- Saving an object: \`localStorage.setItem('obj', {a:1})\` — the string '[object Object]' will be saved.\\nConclusion\\nSerialisation through JSON is a mandatory step when working with localStorage for any data other than simple strings. It guarantees the integrity and the correct type of the data in its subsequent use.","// The saving of an object\\nconst user = { name: 'Alice', age: 30 };\\nlocalStorage.setItem('user', JSON.stringify(user));\\n\\n// The reading and restoration\\nconst storedUser = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem('user'));\\nconsole.log(storedUser.name); // 'Alice'"],[842,"An empty array as the initial state in React can cause superfluous re-renders, because every time the initialiser function is called a new array is created. That breaks referential identity and can lead to endless loops in useEffect. It is better to use null or undefined and to create the array only when necessary.","The problem of an empty array as the initial state\\nIn React, when the \`useState\` hook is used the initial value is passed once, on the first render. If you pass an empty array \`[]\`, then every time the initialiser function is called (during lazy initialisation, for example) a new array object will be created. That breaks the principle of referential identity: React compares states by reference, and a new array is always considered a change.\\nAn example of the problem\\n\\nAs a result the effect fires endlessly, causing superfluous requests or updates. That is especially critical in components with frequent re-renders.\\nThe correct solution\\nUse \`null\` or \`undefined\` as the initial state, and create the array only when necessary:\\n\\nOr use lazy initialisation with a function that returns the array once:\\n\\nConclusion\\nAn empty array as the initial state in React is an anti-pattern, which can lead to unexpected side effects and a reduction in performance. Always use neutral values (null, undefined) or lazy initialisation in order to avoid problems with re-renders.","const [items, setItems] = useState([]); // A new array on every render\\n\\nuseEffect(() => {\\n  // This effect will be carried out on every render,\\n  // because items is a new reference every time\\n  fetchData();\\n}, [items]); // The items dependency always changes\\n\\nconst [items, setItems] = useState(null);\\n\\nuseEffect(() => {\\n  if (items === null) {\\n    fetchData().then(data => setItems(data));\\n  }\\n}, [items]);\\n\\nconst [items, setItems] = useState(() => []); // Called only once"],[843,"To encapsulate the loading, error and data states, a custom hook is used, which manages the asynchronous request. The hook returns an object with the fields data, loading and error. That lets you reuse the loading logic in different components and avoid the duplication of code.","The encapsulation of the states of data loading\\nWhen working with asynchronous requests in React you often have to manage three states: loading, error and the successful data. Encapsulating this logic in a custom hook lets you reuse it in different components, making the code cleaner and more maintainable.\\nAn example of the implementation\\nLet us create a \`useFetch\` hook, which takes a URL and returns an object with the states:\\n\\nThe use in a component\\nThe component simply calls the hook and displays the corresponding state:\\n\\nConclusion\\nEncapsulating the loading states in a custom hook makes managing asynchronous data simpler and improves the readability and the reuse of the code. This approach is recommended in any React application where there are requests to an API.","import { useState, useEffect } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction useFetch(url) {\\n  const [data, setData] = useState(null);\\n  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(true);\\n  const [error, setError] = useState(null);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    const fetchData = async () => {\\n      try {\\n        setLoading(true);\\n        const response = await fetch(url);\\n        if (!response.ok) throw new Error('Network error');\\n        const result = await response.json();\\n        setData(result);\\n      } catch (err) {\\n        setError(err.message);\\n      } finally {\\n        setLoading(false);\\n      }\\n    };\\n    fetchData();\\n  }, [url]);\\n\\n  return { data, loading, error };\\n}\\n\\nfunction UserList() {\\n  const { data, loading, error } = useFetch('/api/users');\\n\\n  if (loading) return <div>Loading...</div>;\\n  if (error) return <div>Error: {error}</div>;\\n  return <ul>{data.map(user => <li key={user.id}>{user.name}</li>)}</ul>;\\n}"],[844,"Memoisation in React (React.memo, useMemo, useCallback) works on the basis of a shallow comparison. When objects are passed, a new object with a new reference is created every time, so the comparison shows that the object has changed and the memoisation does not fire. To solve it you have to stabilise the references by means of useMemo or useCallback, or pass primitive values.","Why memoisation does not work with objects\\nMemoisation in React is an optimisation technique that prevents repeated renders or computations if the input data has not changed. It is based on a shallow comparison, however — that is, the references to objects are compared rather than their content. If a new object is created on every render, its reference will differ from the previous one, and the memoisation will consider that the data has changed.\\nAn example of the problem\\nLet us look at a component that uses React.memo:\\n\\nEvery time Parent renders, a new data object with a new reference is created. React.memo sees that the data prop has changed (the reference is different) and repaints Child, despite the fact that the content of the object is identical.\\nHow to fix it\\nFor the memoisation to work, the reference to the object has to be stabilised. For that use useMemo or useCallback:\\n\\nNow the data object will be created once and will have the same reference on the subsequent renders, if the dependencies do not change.\\nConclusion\\nMemoisation with objects requires stable references. Use useMemo for objects and useCallback for functions, in order to avoid superfluous renders. If an object depends on props or state, include them in the array of dependencies — then the memoisation will update correctly when the data changes.","const Child = React.memo(({ data }) => {\\n  console.log('Child rendered');\\n  return <div>{data.value}</div>;\\n});\\n\\nfunction Parent() {\\n  const data = { value: 'test' }; // a new object on every render\\n  return <Child data={data} />;\\n}\\n\\nfunction Parent() {\\n  const data = useMemo(() => ({ value: 'test' }), []);\\n  return <Child data={data} />;\\n}"],[845,"Event delegation is the approach in which an event handler is set on the parent element instead of on every child element. That lets you handle the events happening on any child element, even if they are added dynamically. For example:\\n\\n\`document.getElementById('parent').addEventListener('click', function(event) {\\nif (event.target.matches('.child')) {\\nalert('A child element has been pressed!');\\n}\\n});\`\\nIn this code the event handler on the parent element reacts to clicks on the child elements with the class \`.child\`.","Event delegation is a technique that lets you handle events on parent elements instead of assigning handlers on every child element. That is useful when you have a multitude of elements and want to minimise the number of event handlers, which improves performance and makes managing events simpler.\\nWhen you assign an event handler on a parent element, it \\"catches\\" the events happening on all of its child elements. Suppose you have a list, for example, and you want to handle clicks on every item of the list:\\n\\nYou can set an event handler on the parent \`<ul>\` element:\\n\\nIn this code, when the user presses any item of the list with the class \`.child\`, the event handler will carry out the code inside the function. Using \`event.target\`, you can gain access to the specific element that was pressed.\\nThis approach is also useful for dynamically created elements. If you add a new list item to the DOM, for example, you will not have to add a new handler, because the parent element is already tracking the clicks on its child elements.\\nThat makes managing events simpler and helps avoid problems with performance, especially when there is a large number of elements on a page.","<ul id=\\"parent\\">\\n  <li class=\\"child\\">Item 1</li>\\n  <li class=\\"child\\">Item 2</li>    \\n  <li class=\\"child\\">Item 3</li> \\n</ul>\\n\\ndocument.getElementById('parent').addEventListener('click', function(event) {\\n  if (event.target.matches('.child')) {    \\n    alert('A child element has been pressed: ' + event.target.textContent);    \\n  } \\n});"],[846,"The main errors when working with localStorage include exceeding the limit of the store (usually 5-10 MB), which raises a QuotaExceededError exception. Problems of security can also arise, such as XSS attacks, since the data is held in the open. Besides that, localStorage does not support automatic synchronisation between tabs, and the data can be lost when the browser's cache is cleared.","The main errors when working with localStorage\\nlocalStorage is a mechanism for holding data on the client's side in the browser. Despite its simplicity, it has a number of limitations and potential problems that it matters to take into account during development.\\nExceeding the limit of the store\\nMost browsers establish a limit on the size of the data in localStorage (usually 5-10 MB per domain). On an attempt to save data beyond the limit, a \`QuotaExceededError\` exception arises. That is especially relevant for applications that hold large volumes of data — a cache of images or logs, for example.\\n\\nProblems of security\\nThe data in localStorage is held in the open and is accessible to any JavaScript code on the page. That makes it vulnerable to XSS attacks. An attacker can gain access to sensitive information such as authentication tokens or the user's settings. Never hold passwords, API keys or other confidential data in localStorage.\\nThe absence of synchronisation between tabs\\nChanges in localStorage in one tab are not automatically reflected in the other tabs of the same domain. For synchronisation it is necessary to use the \`storage\` event, which fires when the data changes in another tab.\\n\\nThe loss of data when the cache is cleared\\nThe user can clear the browser's data, localStorage included, at any moment. That will lead to the loss of all of the data saved. localStorage is therefore unsuitable for holding critically important information that has to be preserved permanently.\\nConclusion\\nlocalStorage is convenient for holding small volumes of non-critical data, such as the settings of the interface or temporary states. To hold sensitive information or data requiring synchronisation, however, it is better to use server-side solutions or more secure mechanisms such as sessionStorage or IndexedDB.","try {\\n  localStorage.setItem('key', 'largeData');\\n} catch (e) {\\n  if (e.name === 'QuotaExceededError') {\\n    console.error('The limit of localStorage has been exceeded');\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nwindow.addEventListener('storage', (event) => {\\n  console.log('The data has been changed:', event.key, event.newValue);\\n});"],[847,"localStorage lets you hold data in the browser with no period of validity. To save, the method setItem('key', 'value') is used. The data is held in the form of strings, so objects have to be converted through JSON.stringify(). Access to the data is possible from any window of the same origin.","What is localStorage and how do you work with it?\\nlocalStorage is part of the Web Storage API, providing the ability to hold key-value pairs in the browser. The data is preserved even after the tab or the browser has been closed and has no period of validity. That is convenient for holding the user's settings, the state of the interface or the caching of small volumes of data.\\nThe saving of data\\nThe main method for writing is \`setItem(key, value)\`. Both parameters must be strings. If you have to save an object or an array, use \`JSON.stringify()\` to convert it into a string.\\n\\nReading and deletion\\nTo obtain data use \`getItem(key)\`, which returns a string or \`null\` if the key is absent. To delete one element — \`removeItem(key)\`; for a full clearance — \`clear()\`.\\n\\nImportant peculiarities\\n\\n- The data is held in the form of strings — any non-string types have to be converted explicitly.\\n- The size of the store is limited (usually 5–10 MB per domain).\\n- localStorage is synchronous — the operations can block the main thread, so do not use it for large volumes of data.\\n- The data is available only for the same protocol, domain and port (the same-origin policy).\\nConclusion\\nlocalStorage is a simple, effective way of holding small volumes of data on the client's side. Use it to save the user's settings, the state of the interface or to cache data that requires no server synchronisation. For more complex scenarios (large volumes, work with files) consider IndexedDB or server-side storage.","// We save a string\\nlocalStorage.setItem('username', 'Alice');\\n\\n// We save an object\\nconst user = { name: 'Bob', age: 30 };\\nlocalStorage.setItem('user', JSON.stringify(user));\\n\\n// We save an array\\nconst colors = ['red', 'green', 'blue'];\\nlocalStorage.setItem('colors', JSON.stringify(colors));\\n\\n// We read the string\\nconst name = localStorage.getItem('username');\\n\\n// We read the object (not forgetting to parse it)\\nconst savedUser = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem('user'));\\n\\n// We delete one element\\nlocalStorage.removeItem('colors');\\n\\n// We clear the whole store\\nlocalStorage.clear();"],[848,"undefined in JavaScript means that a variable has been declared but has no value. Ignoring undefined can lead to errors such as a TypeError on an attempt to access properties. It matters to check values for undefined before using them, especially when working with an API or with user input. That helps you write more reliable, predictable code.","What is undefined and why does it matter?\\nIn JavaScript \`undefined\` is a primitive type, which is automatically assigned to variables declared without initialisation, or returned from functions that have no explicit \`return\`. \`undefined\` also arises when a non-existent property of an object is accessed. Ignoring this state can lead to unexpected errors, especially in a dynamically typed language where the types are checked at runtime.\\nExamples of how undefined arises\\n\\n- A variable has been declared but not initialised: \`let x; console.log(x); // undefined\`\\n- A function without a return: \`function foo() {} console.log(foo()); // undefined\`\\n- A non-existent property of an object: \`const obj = {}; console.log(obj.name); // undefined\`\\nA practical example of handling undefined\\n\\nIn this example we use short-circuiting (\`user && user.name\`) in order to avoid an error on an attempt to access the \`name\` property of \`undefined\` or \`null\`. That is a widespread pattern of defensive programming.\\nWhere it is applied\\nThe handling of \`undefined\` is critical when working with data from external sources (APIs, databases), when handling user input, and also in functions with optional parameters. In TypeScript you can use strict typing, so that the compiler warns about possible \`undefined\` values.\\nConclusion: Taking \`undefined\` into account as a possible state of data helps you avoid runtime errors and makes code more resilient. Always check values for \`undefined\` before using them, especially in dynamic environments.","function getUserName(user) {\\n  // The check for undefined and null\\n  if (user && user.name) {\\n    return user.name;\\n  }\\n  return 'Anonymous';\\n}\\n\\nconst user1 = { name: 'Alice' };\\nconst user2 = {};\\nconst user3 = null;\\n\\nconsole.log(getUserName(user1)); // Alice\\nconsole.log(getUserName(user2)); // Anonymous\\nconsole.log(getUserName(user3)); // Anonymous"],[849,"It is better to pass primitives, since they are compared by value and do not cause superfluous re-renders when React.memo is used. Objects are created anew on every render, which breaks the memoisation. If an object is needed, it should be stabilised through useMemo or moved outside the component.","The influence of the type of the props on re-renders\\nIn React, on every update of a component all of its child elements are re-rendered by default. For optimisation React.memo is used, which compares the new and the old props. Primitives (numbers, strings, boolean values) are compared by value — if the value has not changed, the component will not re-render. Objects, on the other hand, are compared by reference, and on every render of the parent a new object is created, even if its content is identical.\\nAn example with primitives\\n\\nHere Child will re-render only when count changes.\\nThe problem with objects\\n\\nChild will re-render on every click, even if data has not changed.\\nHow to pass objects correctly\\n\\n- Use useMemo to stabilise the reference:\\n\\n- Move the object outside the component, if it is static:\\n\\n- Divide the object into separate primitive props, if that is possible.\\nConclusion: for maximum performance and the predictable behaviour of the memoisation it is preferable to pass primitives. If an object is necessary, it has to be stabilised by means of useMemo or moved outside the component.","const Child = React.memo(({ count }) => {\\n  console.log('Child render');\\n  return <span>{count}</span>;\\n});\\n\\nfunction Parent() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n  return (\\n    <>\\n      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>+</button>\\n      <Child count={count} />\\n    </>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nconst Child = React.memo(({ data }) => {\\n  console.log('Child render');\\n  return <span>{data.value}</span>;\\n});\\n\\nfunction Parent() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n  const data = { value: 'test' }; // a new object every render\\n  return (\\n    <>\\n      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>+</button>\\n      <Child data={data} />\\n    </>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nconst data = useMemo(() => ({ value: 'test' }), []);\\n\\nconst DATA = { value: 'test' };"],[850,"The handling of API errors includes checking the HTTP statuses, the use of try-catch for exceptions and the implementation of retries on temporary failures. It matters to log the errors and show the user understandable messages. For asynchronous requests use async/await with the handling of a reject.","The basics of handling API errors\\nWhen working with an API errors are inevitable: the server may be unavailable, the request may be incorrect or the data may be absent. The correct handling of errors makes an application resilient and improves the user experience.\\nChecking the HTTP statuses\\nEvery API response contains an HTTP status, which points to success or failure. For example, 200 OK — success, 404 Not Found — the resource has not been found, 500 Internal Server Error — an error of the server. In the code you have to check the status and react accordingly.\\n\\nThe use of try-catch with async/await\\nThe modern approach is to use async/await with a try-catch block to handle the errors of asynchronous requests. That makes the code more readable.\\n\\nRetries\\nTemporary errors (503 Service Unavailable, for example) can be handled by means of retries with a delay. That raises reliability.\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe handling of API errors is mandatory for building reliable applications. Use the checking of the statuses, try-catch and retry mechanisms, in order to minimise the influence of failures and ensure correct feedback to the user.","fetch('/api/data')\\n  .then(response => {\\n    if (!response.ok) {\\n      throw new Error(\`HTTP error! status: \${response.status}\`);\\n    }\\n    return response.json();\\n  })\\n  .catch(error => console.error('Fetch error:', error));\\n\\nasync function getData() {\\n  try {\\n    const response = await fetch('/api/data');\\n    if (!response.ok) {\\n      throw new Error(\`HTTP error! status: \${response.status}\`);\\n    }\\n    const data = await response.json();\\n    return data;\\n  } catch (error) {\\n    console.error('Failed to fetch data:', error);\\n    throw error; // we throw it further\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nasync function fetchWithRetry(url, retries = 3) {\\n  for (let i = 0; i < retries; i++) {\\n    try {\\n      const response = await fetch(url);\\n      if (response.ok) return response.json();\\n      if (response.status >= 500 && i < retries - 1) {\\n        await new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, 1000 * (i + 1)));\\n        continue;\\n      }\\n      throw new Error(\`HTTP error! status: \${response.status}\`);\\n    } catch (error) {\\n      if (i === retries - 1) throw error;\\n    }\\n  }\\n}"],[851,"In useCallback you have to state every variable and function from the outer scope that is used inside the callback. That includes props, states and other values on which the function's working depends. If a dependency is not stated, the callback will use a stale value. React itself hints through the linter which dependencies are needed.","What useCallback is and why the dependencies are needed\\nuseCallback is a React hook that returns a memoised version of a callback. It prevents a new function from being created on every render, if the dependencies have not changed. The array of dependencies — the hook's second argument — determines when the callback should be recreated.\\nWhich dependencies to state\\nIn the array of dependencies you have to include every variable, function, prop and state that is used inside the callback and that can change between renders. If the callback uses a value that is not stated in the dependencies, it will capture a stale value from the closure.\\n\\nThe rules and recommendations\\n\\n- Always state every variable used inside the callback\\n- If the callback uses only the setter functions from useState, the dependencies can be omitted, since they are stable\\n- Use eslint-plugin-react-hooks for the automatic checking of the dependencies\\n- Do not state in the dependencies functions that are themselves memoised through useCallback or useMemo\\nConclusion\\nThe correct stating of the dependencies in useCallback is critically important for an application's correct working. Errors in the dependencies lead to bugs with stale data and to unexpected behaviour. Always follow the rule: state every variable from the outer scope that the callback uses, and use the linter for an automatic check.","import React, { useState, useCallback } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction Counter() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n  const [step, setStep] = useState(1);\\n\\n  // Correct: step is stated in the dependencies\\n  const increment = useCallback(() => {\\n    setCount(prev => prev + step);\\n  }, [step]);\\n\\n  // Incorrect: step is not stated, the old value will be used\\n  const badIncrement = useCallback(() => {\\n    setCount(prev => prev + step);\\n  }, []);\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <p>Count: {count}</p>\\n      <button onClick={increment}>Increment</button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}"],[852,"useCallback is needed when you pass a function as a prop to a child component wrapped in React.memo, or when the function is used in the array of dependencies of other hooks (useEffect, useMemo). If the function is created inside a component and passed into an ordinary HTML element (div, button) or into a component without React.memo, useCallback gives no gain, since the re-render will happen anyway. It is also not worth using useCallback for simple functions called on an event (onClick, onChange), if there are no problems with performance.","When is useCallback needed?\\nuseCallback in React is used to memoise functions. It returns one and the same reference to the function between renders, until the dependencies change. That is useful in two main scenarios:\\n\\n- The passing of a function into a child component wrapped in React.memo. React.memo prevents a re-render if the props have not changed, but without useCallback every new reference to the function will be considered a change of the prop.\\n- The use of the function in the array of dependencies of other hooks (useEffect, useMemo, useCallback). If the function is not memoised, it will be created anew on every render, causing endless loops or superfluous calls of the effects.\\nWhen is useCallback not needed?\\nIf a function is passed into an ordinary HTML element (\`<button onClick={handleClick}>\`, for example), useCallback gives no gain, since the re-render will happen anyway. It is also not worth using it for simple event handlers if there are no measurable problems with performance. Premature optimisation can make the code more complex.\\nA practical example\\n\\nIn this example \`ExpensiveChild\` is wrapped in React.memo. Without useCallback every re-render of Parent would create a new function, and React.memo would not fire. With useCallback the reference to the function is stable, and the child component is not re-rendered unnecessarily.\\nConclusion\\nUse useCallback only when there is a real problem with performance or when the function takes part in the dependencies of other hooks. Do not apply it to every function indiscriminately — that makes the code more complex without benefit.","import React, { useState, useCallback } from 'react';\\n\\nconst ExpensiveChild = React.memo(({ onClick }) => {\\n  console.log('Child re-rendered');\\n  return <button onClick={onClick}>Click</button>;\\n});\\n\\nfunction Parent() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n\\n  // Without useCallback: a new function on every render\\n  const handleClick = useCallback(() => {\\n    setCount(prev => prev + 1);\\n  }, []); // The dependencies are empty, the function is stable\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <p>Count: {count}</p>\\n      <ExpensiveChild onClick={handleClick} />\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}"],[853,"The structure of components in React should be modular and hierarchical. Components divide into presentational ones (responsible for the display) and container ones (which manage the logic and the data). The files are grouped by functional modules or features rather than by types. Every component is held in a separate folder with its own style, tests and an index.js to make importing simpler.","The principles of organising components in React\\nThe correct structure of components is the foundation of a scalable React application. The main idea consists in the separation of responsibility: every component should carry out one task. That makes testing, reuse and the maintenance of the code simpler.\\nThe main approaches\\n\\n- Feature-based grouping: Every piece of an application's functionality (authorisation or the list of products, for example) has its own folder, inside which lie all of the related components, styles, tests and hooks.\\n- The division into presentational and container components: Presentational components are responsible only for the display of the UI, receiving the data through props. Container components manage the state and the business logic, passing the data to the presentational ones.\\n- Atomic Design: Components divide into atoms (buttons, inputs), molecules (forms), organisms (the site's header), templates and pages. That helps create a single design system.\\nAn example of the structure of the folders\\n\\nPractical recommendations\\n\\n- Use an index.js to re-export a component from its folder — that makes the imports simpler.\\n- Avoid deep nesting (more than 3-4 levels).\\n- Move common logic out into custom hooks (useFetch, for example).\\n- For styles use CSS Modules or styled-components, in order to avoid name conflicts.\\nConclusion\\nApply a modular structure based on features for medium and large projects. For small applications a simple grouping by types (components, containers, pages) is enough. The main thing is to observe uniformity and the principle of single responsibility.","src/\\n  components/          # Common reusable components\\n    Button/\\n      index.js\\n      Button.jsx\\n      Button.module.css\\n      Button.test.js\\n  features/            # The application's features\\n    auth/\\n      components/\\n        LoginForm/\\n          index.js\\n          LoginForm.jsx\\n          LoginForm.module.css\\n      hooks/\\n        useAuth.js\\n      api/\\n        authApi.js\\n  pages/               # The pages (the composition of the features)\\n    HomePage.jsx\\n    ProfilePage.jsx\\n  App.jsx\\n  index.js"],[854,"Yes, XML and JSON can be used in various types of request, such as GET, POST, PUT and DELETE. JSON is more often applied in REST APIs because of its lightness and simplicity, whereas XML is used in SOAP and in configuration files. The choice depends on the project's requirements and on compatibility with the systems.","The use of XML and JSON in requests\\nXML and JSON are formats for the exchange of data, which can be used in various types of HTTP request. JSON (JavaScript Object Notation) is more popular in modern web applications thanks to its compactness and its convenience for JavaScript. XML (eXtensible Markup Language) is often applied in corporate systems and in protocols such as SOAP.\\nExamples of use\\nIn a REST API, JSON is usually passed in the body of POST, PUT or PATCH requests, and can also be part of the response to a GET request. XML can also be used in the same way, but it requires additional handling on the client's and the server's side.\\n\\nConclusion\\nXML and JSON can be used in any type of request, but JSON is preferable for lightweight, fast applications, and XML for systems requiring a strict structure and extensibility — in SOAP services, for example.","// An example of JSON in a POST request\\nfetch('/api/users', {\\n  method: 'POST',\\n  headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },\\n  body: JSON.stringify({ name: 'John', age: 30 })\\n});\\n\\n// An example of XML in a POST request (using SOAP)\\nconst xml = '<?xml version=\\"1.0\\"?>' +\\n  '<soap:Envelope xmlns:soap=\\"http://schemas.xmlsoap.org/soap/envelope/\\">' +\\n  '<soap:Body><getUser><id>123</id></getUser></soap:Body>' +\\n  '</soap:Envelope>';\\nfetch('/api/soap', {\\n  method: 'POST',\\n  headers: { 'Content-Type': 'text/xml' },\\n  body: xml\\n});"],[855,"To test the withdrawal of a sum less than the minimum you have to write a unit test that calls the withdrawal method with the sum of 1 rouble. The expectation is that the system will throw an exception or return an error, since the sum is below the minimum threshold. The test checks that the balance has not changed and that the error has been handled correctly.","Boundary-value testing when withdrawing funds\\nWhen developing financial systems it matters to check how the application reacts to the entry of incorrect data, such as a sum less than the minimum permissible. That belongs to boundary value testing — one of the key methods of ensuring quality. In this case the minimum sum for withdrawal is, say, 100 roubles, while the sum being tested is 1 rouble, which is a value below the boundary.\\nAn example of the implementation in JavaScript\\nLet us look at a bank-account class with a withdraw method. The test checks that on an attempt to withdraw 1 rouble an exception is thrown and the balance stays unchanged.\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\nSuch an approach is used in any system with restrictions: banking applications, online shops (a minimum order sum), APIs with the validation of parameters. Boundary-value testing helps reveal errors at the early stages and prevent incorrect behaviour in production.\\nConclusion: Testing the withdrawal of a sum less than the minimum is necessary to check the correctness of the validation and of the handling of exceptions. It is a mandatory part of the test coverage for systems with financial operations, where errors can lead to serious consequences.","class BankAccount {\\n  constructor(balance, minWithdraw) {\\n    this.balance = balance;\\n    this.minWithdraw = minWithdraw;\\n  }\\n\\n  withdraw(amount) {\\n    if (amount < this.minWithdraw) {\\n      throw new Error(\`The sum is less than the minimum: \${this.minWithdraw}\`);\\n    }\\n    this.balance -= amount;\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// The test\\ntest('the withdrawal of a sum less than the minimum', () => {\\n  const account = new BankAccount(500, 100);\\n  expect(() => account.withdraw(1)).toThrow();\\n  expect(account.balance).toBe(500);\\n});"],[856,"The event object in JavaScript represents information about an event that happened on a web page — a mouse click or a key press, for example. It contains properties that describe that event, such as the type of the event, the target element and the coordinates of the mouse. For example, \`event.type\` returns the type of the event, while \`event.target\` points to the element with which the event happened.","The event object in JavaScript is created every time an event happens on a web page. It contains information about the event that happened and lets developers gain access to the important data connected with that event.\\nSome of the key properties of the event object include:\\n\\n- \`type\`: returns a string stating the type of the event (\`'click'\`, \`'keydown'\` and so on, for example). That lets you determine which event happened.\\n\\n- \`target\`: returns the element on which the event happened. That is useful for carrying out actions only with a specific element.\\n\\n- \`currentTarget\`: returns the element to which the event handler was bound. That can be useful in situations where the handler is attached to a parent element but the event happened on a child one.\\n\\n- \`clientX\` and \`clientY\`: return the coordinates of the mouse cursor relative to the viewport at the event — on a mouse click, for example.\\n\\nBesides that, the event object can contain methods such as \`preventDefault()\`, which cancels the browser's standard behaviour (prevents following a link, for example), and \`stopPropagation()\`, which stops the bubbling of the event.\\nThe use of the event object lets you manage the user's interactions with a web application more effectively.","document.getElementById('myButton').addEventListener('click', function(event) {\\n  console.log(event.type); // 'click' \\n});\\n\\ndocument.getElementById('myButton').addEventListener('click', function(event) {\\n  console.log(event.target); // the button element \\n});"],[857,"Open the developer tools in the browser (F12). Check the console for JavaScript errors. Make sure an event handler has been correctly assigned to the button. Check the Network tab in order to see whether requests are being sent to the server. If there are no errors, check whether the script is blocked or whether the event is being intercepted.","The diagnosis of the problem\\nWhen nothing happens after a button is pressed, the first thing to do is to open the browser's developer tools (usually the F12 key). It is the main tool for debugging web applications.\\nChecking the console\\nGo to the Console tab. Here every JavaScript error, warning and log is displayed. If there is an error, it usually contains a description and the number of the line of code. The error 'Uncaught TypeError: Cannot read properties of null', for example, points to the fact that you are trying to access a non-existent DOM element.\\nChecking the event handler\\nMake sure an event handler has been assigned to the button. That can be done through the onclick attribute in the HTML or through addEventListener in JavaScript. An example of correct assignment:\\n\\nChecking the network requests\\nIf the event handler is there but nothing happens, go to the Network tab. Press the button and see whether new requests appear. If there are no requests, then the problem is in the client code. If there is a request, check its status and the server's response.\\nOther possible causes\\n\\n- The script did not load because of an error somewhere else in the code\\n- The event is being intercepted by another handler (event.stopPropagation)\\n- The button is inside a form and the form is being submitted instead of the handler being called\\n- A browser extension is blocking the execution of the script\\nConclusion\\nA systematic approach to debugging — checking the console, the event handlers and the network requests — lets you reveal most problems quickly. Use the developer tools as the main tool for the diagnosis.","// HTML: <button id=\\"myButton\\">Click me</button>\\n// JavaScript:\\ndocument.getElementById('myButton').addEventListener('click', function() {\\n  console.log('Button clicked!');\\n  // Your code\\n});"],[858,"Check the browser's console for JavaScript errors and the network requests. Make sure the server returned a correct response (status 200). Check whether the response is being handled in the code and whether the state of the interface is being updated. It is also worth checking whether the response is being blocked by CORS or by other security policies.","The diagnosis of the problem of the absence of a result after a request has been sent\\nWhen a user sends a request but does not see a result, it is necessary to check several levels systematically: the client part, the network and the server part. Most often the problem lies in JavaScript errors, in the incorrect handling of the response or in network restrictions.\\nThe check on the client's side\\n\\n- Open the browser's developer tools (F12) and go to the Console tab. Look for JavaScript errors such as a TypeError or a ReferenceError.\\n- On the Network tab check whether the request was sent (the method, the URL, the headers). Look at the status of the response: 200 — success, 4xx — a client error, 5xx — a server error.\\n- Make sure the response contains the data expected. If the response is empty or has an error, that may be the cause.\\nChecking the handling of the response in the code\\nEven if the server returned correct data, the client code may not display it. Check that you are handling the response correctly and updating the state of the interface.\\n\\nChecking the server part\\n\\n- Make sure the server handled the request and returned a response. Check the server's logs for errors.\\n- Check whether the request is being blocked by CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing). If the request is sent to another domain, the server has to permit it with headers.\\nAdditional checks\\n\\n- Check whether the response is being cached by the browser. Sometimes old data can hide the current result.\\n- Make sure the user is authorised and has the rights to carry out the request.\\nConclusion: the systematic checking of the console, the network requests and the handling of the response helps you reveal the cause of the absence of a result quickly. This approach is applicable in any web application for debugging the interaction of the client and the server.","fetch('/api/data')\\n  .then(response => response.json())\\n  .then(data => {\\n    // Check that data is not undefined and contains the fields needed\\n    if (data && data.result) {\\n      updateUI(data.result);\\n    } else {\\n      console.error('An incorrect response:', data);\\n    }\\n  })\\n  .catch(error => console.error('An error of the request:', error));"],[859,"Client-server architecture is a model where the client sends requests and the server handles them and returns a response. The client is the user's device or program, while the server is a powerful computer with the data and the logic. It is the basis of the working of web sites, applications and databases.","What is client-server architecture?\\nClient-server architecture is a model of distributed computing in which the tasks are divided between the providers of services (the servers) and their consumers (the clients). The client initiates the connection by sending a request, while the server responds to it, providing data or carrying out actions. It is a fundamental concept for building scalable, manageable network applications.\\nHow does it work?\\nThe interaction happens over a protocol, HTTP for example. The client (a browser, a mobile application) sends a request to the server. The server handles the request, turns to the database or to other resources and returns a response (an HTML page, JSON data).\\nAn example of code (a simple HTTP server in Node.js)\\n\\nThe client (curl localhost:3000, for example) sends the request, and the server responds with the string \\"Hello, client!\\".\\nWhere is it applied?\\n\\n- Web applications (a browser + a web server)\\n- Databases (an application + a DBMS)\\n- Electronic mail (a mail client + a server)\\nConclusion: Client-server architecture is used everywhere the centralised management of data and logic is needed, ensuring security, scalability and convenience of servicing.","const http = require('http');\\n\\nconst server = http.createServer((req, res) => {\\n  res.statusCode = 200;\\n  res.setHeader('Content-Type', 'text/plain');\\n  res.end('Hello, client!');\\n});\\n\\nserver.listen(3000, () => {\\n  console.log('Server running at http://localhost:3000/');\\n});"],[860,"In JSON, quotation marks inside strings are escaped with a backslash. A string with quotation marks looks like this: \\"He said: \\\\\\"Hello\\\\\\"\\". The backslash, the control characters (\\\\n, \\\\t) and some others are also escaped. That is necessary for the correct parsing of JSON.","The escaping of quotation marks in JSON\\nJSON uses double quotation marks to denote strings. If a double quotation mark has to be used inside a string, it has to be escaped with a backslash (\`\\\\\\\\\`). That lets the parser distinguish the service quotation mark from a character inside the string.\\nThe main rules of escaping\\n\\n- A double quotation mark: \`\\\\\\"\`\\n- A backslash: \`\\\\\\\\\`\\n- The control characters: \`\\\\n\` (a new line), \`\\\\t\` (a tab), \`\\\\r\` (a carriage return)\\n- Other characters: \`\\\\/\` (a forward slash, optional), \`\\\\b\` (a backspace), \`\\\\f\` (a form feed)\\nAn example\\n\\nIn this example the string contains double quotation marks, which have been escaped. Without the escaping the JSON would be invalid.\\nConclusion\\nThe escaping of quotation marks is mandatory for creating correct JSON data. That is especially important when generating JSON in code or writing configuration files by hand.","{\\n  \\"message\\": \\"He said: \\\\\\"Hello, world!\\\\\\"\\"\\n}"],[861,"JSON requires a strict syntax: keys and strings in double quotation marks, numbers without quotation marks, the logical values true/false, null. The data is separated by commas, objects are in curly brackets and arrays in square ones. Trailing commas and comments are not permitted.","The main rules of JSON syntax\\nJSON (JavaScript Object Notation) is a lightweight data-exchange format based on a subset of JavaScript. Its syntax is strict and requires the following rules to be observed:\\n\\n- Keys and strings are always enclosed in double quotation marks (\`\\"\`). Single quotation marks are not permitted.\\n- Numbers are written without quotation marks and can be whole or floating-point (\`42\`, \`3.14\`, for example).\\n- The logical values are \`true\` and \`false\` (without quotation marks).\\n- Null is \`null\` (without quotation marks).\\n- Objects are enclosed in curly brackets \`{}\`, and the key-value pairs are separated by commas.\\n- Arrays are enclosed in square brackets \`[]\`, and the elements are separated by commas.\\n- Trailing commas (a comma after the last element) are forbidden.\\n- Comments are not supported.\\nAn example of correct JSON\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\nJSON is used in APIs, configuration files, the holding of data and the exchange between a client and a server. The observance of the syntax is mandatory for parsing and serialisation.\\nConclusion: JSON is a simple, strict format, which is easily read and handled. Its rules of syntax ensure unambiguity and compatibility between systems.","{\\n  \\"name\\": \\"Alice\\",\\n  \\"age\\": 30,\\n  \\"isStudent\\": false,\\n  \\"hobbies\\": [\\"reading\\", \\"coding\\"],\\n  \\"address\\": null\\n}"],[862,"Errors in JSON can be syntactic: superfluous commas, incorrect quotation marks (only double ones), the absence of quotation marks on keys, incorrect data types (undefined, for example). Errors during parsing are also possible, if the string does not conform to the format. Problems with the escaping of characters and with nesting are often met.","The main errors in JSON\\nJSON (JavaScript Object Notation) is a textual data-exchange format based on the syntax of JavaScript. It is stricter, however: the keys and the strings must be in double quotation marks, and the values only of particular types (a string, a number, an object, an array, a boolean, null).\\nSyntactic errors\\n\\n- Superfluous commas: a comma after the last element in an object or an array is not permitted. An example: \`{\\"a\\": 1,}\` — an error.\\n- Incorrect quotation marks: only double quotation marks (\`\\"\`). Single ones (\`'\`) or backticks will raise an error.\\n- The absence of quotation marks on keys: the keys must be strings in quotation marks. \`{key: \\"value\\"}\` is incorrect; \`{\\"key\\": \\"value\\"}\` is needed.\\n- Impermissible values: \`undefined\`, \`NaN\` and \`Infinity\` are not supported. Use \`null\`.\\n- Errors of escaping: special characters (a quotation mark inside a string, for example) have to be escaped with a backslash: \`\\\\\\"\`.\\nExamples of code\\n\\nConclusion\\nA knowledge of the typical errors of JSON helps you avoid problems when passing data between a client and a server, and also when working with configuration files. Always check JSON with a validator and use try-catch when parsing.","// Incorrect JSON\\nconst badJson = '{\\"name\\": \\"John\\", \\"age\\": undefined}';\\n// Correct JSON\\nconst goodJson = '{\\"name\\": \\"John\\", \\"age\\": null}';\\n\\n// Parsing with the handling of errors\\ntry {\\n  const data = JSON.parse(badJson);\\n} catch (e) {\\n  console.error('An error of parsing:', e.message);\\n}"],[863,"For the automation of testing various languages are used, such as Python, Java, JavaScript, C# and Ruby. Python is popular thanks to its simplicity and libraries of the PyTest and Selenium kind. Java is often applied with JUnit and TestNG, while JavaScript is applied with Jest and Cypress. The choice of language depends on the project's technology stack and on the requirements as regards the tests.","The main languages for the automation of testing\\nThe automation of testing lets you speed up the checking of code and raise its quality. The choice of programming language for writing the tests depends on the project's ecosystem, the team's experience and the type of testing (unit tests, integration, E2E). The most widespread languages include Python, Java, JavaScript, C# and Ruby.\\nPython\\nPython is one of the most popular languages for automation thanks to its readability and its rich library. For unit tests PyTest or unittest is used, for UI tests Selenium WebDriver, and for API tests requests. An example of a simple test in PyTest:\\n\\nJava\\nJava is widely applied in corporate projects. The main frameworks: JUnit and TestNG for unit tests, Selenium for UI, RestAssured for APIs. An example:\\n\\nJavaScript\\nJavaScript is popular in web development. For testing, Jest (React), Mocha, Cypress (E2E) and Playwright are used. An example with Jest:\\n\\nC# and Ruby\\nC# is used with NUnit or xUnit in .NET projects, while Ruby is used with RSpec and Capybara for web tests. The choice depends on the platform.\\nConclusion: The language for the automation of testing is chosen on the basis of the project's technology stack and the tools available. Python and JavaScript suit fast prototypes, Java and C# large systems, and Ruby web applications.","import pytest\\n\\ndef test_addition():\\n    assert 1 + 1 == 2\\n\\nimport org.junit.Test;\\nimport static org.junit.Assert.*;\\n\\npublic class CalculatorTest {\\n    @Test\\n    public void testAdd() {\\n        assertEquals(2, 1 + 1);\\n    }\\n}\\n\\ntest('adds 1 + 2 to equal 3', () => {\\n    expect(1 + 2).toBe(3);\\n});"],[864,"JSON supports six data types: a string, a number, a boolean value, null, an array and an object. Strings are always in double quotation marks, numbers can be whole or floating-point, and arrays and objects can be nested. Other types, such as dates or undefined, are not supported directly.","The main data types of JSON\\nJSON (JavaScript Object Notation) is a lightweight data-exchange format based on a subset of the syntax of JavaScript. It supports exactly six data types, which let you represent practically any structured information.\\n\\n- String — a sequence of characters in double quotation marks; it supports escape sequences (\`\\\\n\`, \`\\\\t\`, for example). An example: \\"Hello, world!\\".\\n- Number — a whole or floating-point number, which can be negative. It does not support NaN, Infinity or leading zeroes. An example: 42, 3.14, -7.\\n- Boolean — true or false.\\n- Null — a special value denoting the absence of data. Written as null.\\n- Array — an ordered list of values, enclosed in square brackets. The elements can be of any type, including other arrays and objects. An example: [1, \\"two\\", false].\\n- Object — an unordered collection of key-value pairs, enclosed in curly brackets. The keys are always strings in double quotation marks. An example: {\\"name\\": \\"Alice\\", \\"age\\": 30}.\\nAn example of use\\n\\nThe limitations\\nJSON does not support dates, functions, undefined, symbols or binary data. To pass dates, strings in the ISO 8601 format are usually used. There is also no support for comments — that was done for the simplicity of parsing.\\nConclusion\\nA knowledge of the data types of JSON is necessary for the correct serialisation and deserialisation of data when exchanging it between a client and a server, and also for working with configuration files and APIs.","{\\n  \\"user\\": {\\n    \\"id\\": 123,\\n    \\"name\\": \\"John Doe\\",\\n    \\"active\\": true,\\n    \\"roles\\": [\\"admin\\", \\"editor\\"],\\n    \\"metadata\\": null\\n  }\\n}"],[865,"Playwright is a library for the automation of browsers, developed by Microsoft. It lets you control Chromium, Firefox and WebKit through a single API. It is applied for the end-to-end testing of web applications, for scraping and for the automation of actions in the browser. It supports several programming languages, including JavaScript, TypeScript, Python and C#.","What is Playwright?\\nPlaywright is a modern open-source library for the automation of browsers, created by the Microsoft team. It provides a single API for controlling the three main browser engines: Chromium, Firefox and WebKit. That lets developers and testers write scenarios that work in every popular browser without changing the code.\\nThe main capabilities\\n\\n- The automation of the user's actions: clicks, the entry of text, navigation.\\n- Waiting for elements and states of the page without explicit delays.\\n- Work with several tabs and windows.\\n- The interception and modification of network requests.\\n- The emulation of mobile devices and of geolocation.\\nAn example of use\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\nPlaywright is widely used in the end-to-end testing of web applications, especially in CI/CD pipelines. It also suits web scraping, the automation of repeating tasks and the testing of performance. Thanks to its support for several languages, it can be integrated into projects in JavaScript, TypeScript, Python, Java and .NET.\\nConclusion: Playwright is a powerful tool for the automation of browsers, which is worth applying when reliable cross-browser testing or the automation of complex user scenarios in web applications is required.","const { chromium } = require('playwright');\\n\\n(async () => {\\n  const browser = await chromium.launch();\\n  const page = await browser.newPage();\\n  await page.goto('https://example.com');\\n  await page.fill('#search', 'Playwright');\\n  await page.click('button[type=\\"submit\\"]');\\n  const title = await page.title();\\n  console.log(title);\\n  await browser.close();\\n})();"],[866,"A HAR file (HTTP Archive) is a JSON file that records every network request and response of the browser. It is used to analyse performance, debug the loading of pages and reproduce problems with the network. Developers export a HAR from the browser's developer tools in order to pass the data to colleagues or testers.","What is a HAR file?\\nHAR (HTTP Archive) is a JSON-based file format that holds detailed information about every network interaction between the browser and the server while a web page is loading. It includes data about every request: the URL, the headers, the execution time, the size of the response, the status code and much more.\\nWhat is HAR used for?\\nHAR files are applied for:\\n\\n- The diagnosis of slow page loading — you can see which resources take the longest to load.\\n- The debugging of network errors — 404 or 500 statuses, for example.\\n- The reproduction of problems in a development or testing environment.\\n- The analysis of the sequence of the requests and of the dependencies between them.\\nAn example of creating a HAR in the browser\\nIn Chrome open DevTools (F12), go to the Network tab, refresh the page, then right-click on any request and choose \\"Save all as HAR with content\\".\\nAn example of the content of a HAR (simplified)\\n\\nConclusion\\nHAR files are a powerful tool for analysing performance and debugging network problems. They are worth using when optimising the loading of sites, looking for bottlenecks in an API or reproducing errors connected with the network.","{\\n  \\"log\\": {\\n    \\"version\\": \\"1.2\\",\\n    \\"entries\\": [\\n      {\\n        \\"request\\": {\\n          \\"method\\": \\"GET\\",\\n          \\"url\\": \\"https://example.com/api/data\\",\\n          \\"headers\\": [{\\"name\\": \\"Accept\\", \\"value\\": \\"application/json\\"}]\\n        },\\n        \\"response\\": {\\n          \\"status\\": 200,\\n          \\"content\\": {\\"text\\": \\"{\\\\\\"key\\\\\\": \\\\\\"value\\\\\\"}\\"}\\n        },\\n        \\"timings\\": {\\n          \\"wait\\": 150,\\n          \\"receive\\": 30\\n        }\\n      }\\n    ]\\n  }\\n}"],[867,"The removal of event handlers is necessary to manage performance and prevent memory leaks in an application. If an event handler is no longer needed, it should be removed, in order to avoid unnecessary calls of functions and to save resources. Besides that, the removal of handlers prevents the execution of code that may be connected with elements that have already been removed from the DOM.","The removal of event handlers is an important aspect of managing memory and performance in web applications. When you add an event handler, it takes up memory and resources. If an event handler stays active even after the element has been removed or is no longer used, that can lead to memory leaks, since the references to those elements are still preserved and JavaScript cannot free them.\\nAlso, if a handler stays active, it can continue calling functions even if the user no longer interacts with the corresponding element. That can lead to unpredictable behaviour of the application and a reduction in performance.\\nAn example of removing an event handler:\\n\\nIn this example, after \`removeEventListener()\` has been called the handler will no longer be called when the button is clicked. That is useful when the element is no longer needed — if it has been removed from the DOM, for example, or if you want to switch the handling of events off temporarily.\\nThe removal of event handlers helps maintain the cleanliness of the code, avoid undesirable consequences and optimise the performance of web applications.","function handleClick(event) {\\n  console.log('The button has been pressed!'); \\n} \\n// We add the event handler \\ndocument.getElementById('myButton').addEventListener('click', handleClick); \\n// We remove the event handler \\ndocument.getElementById('myButton').removeEventListener('click', handleClick);"],[868,"Configuration values are held in environment variables or in special configuration files. Environment variables are set outside the code and are accessible through process.env. .env files are convenient for local development, but they should not reach the repository. For different environments (dev, prod) different sets of configurations are used.","Why configuration is needed\\nConfiguration values are parameters that can change depending on the environment: the URL of the database, API keys, the server's ports. Holding them in the code is bad practice, since it breaks security and flexibility.\\nThe main approaches\\n\\n- Environment variables — they are set in the operating system or the container and are accessible through \`process.env\` in Node.js. An example: \`process.env.DB_URL\`.\\n- .env files — for local development. The \`dotenv\` library loads them into \`process.env\`. The \`.env\` file is added to \`.gitignore\`.\\n- Configuration files — JSON, YAML or JS modules, which are imported into the code. Often used for complex structures.\\nAn example of code\\n\\nRecommendations\\n\\n- Do not hold secrets in the repository — use environment variables on the server.\\n- For different environments create separate files: \`.env.development\`, \`.env.production\`.\\n- Validate the mandatory variables when the application starts.\\nConclusion: Use environment variables for sensitive data and configuration files for static parameters. That makes an application secure and easily portable between environments.","// The installation: npm install dotenv\\nrequire('dotenv').config();\\n\\nconst dbUrl = process.env.DB_URL || 'localhost:27017';\\nconst port = process.env.PORT || 3000;\\n\\nconsole.log(\`The server has started on the port \${port}\`);"],[869,"SQL Injection is an attack in which an attacker injects malicious SQL code into a query through user input. That can lead to the theft of data, to its change or to its deletion. You can protect yourself by means of parameterised queries (prepared statements), the use of an ORM, the escaping of input and the restriction of the database's rights.","What is SQL Injection?\\nSQL Injection is a type of attack on web applications in which an attacker inserts or \\"injects\\" malicious SQL code into a query to a database. It happens when an application directly concatenates user input with an SQL query without due handling. On a login page, for example, an attacker can enter something like \`' OR '1'='1\` in the login field, which will turn the query into \`SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = '' OR '1'='1' AND password = '...'\`, and the condition will always be true, letting them bypass the authentication.\\nHow do you protect yourself?\\nThe main method of protection is the use of parameterised queries (prepared statements). They separate the SQL code from the data by passing the parameters separately, which does not let the input be interpreted as part of the query. The use of an ORM (Object-Relational Mapping), which escapes input automatically, is also effective. In addition it is worth applying the validation and escaping of input, and restricting the rights of the database's user to the minimum necessary.\\nAn example of code\\nVulnerable code in PHP:\\n\\nSafe code with prepared statements:\\n\\nIn the safe variant the question marks are replaced by the values passed, but they are not interpreted as SQL code.\\nConclusion\\nSQL Injection is one of the most dangerous vulnerabilities, but it is easy to prevent by using parameterised queries or an ORM. Always handle user input as data rather than as code, and follow the principle of least privilege for the database.","$username = $_POST['username'];\\n$password = $_POST['password'];\\n$query = \\"SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = '$username' AND password = '$password'\\";\\n$result = mysqli_query($conn, $query);\\n\\n$stmt = $conn->prepare(\\"SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = ? AND password = ?\\");\\n$stmt->bind_param(\\"ss\\", $username, $password);\\n$stmt->execute();\\n$result = $stmt->get_result();"],[870,"No, LAZY loading does not in itself solve the N+1 problem; it is often its cause. The N+1 problem arises when, to obtain a collection of related entities, one query is carried out for the parent entity and N additional queries for every child one. LAZY loading postpones the loading of the relations until the moment they are accessed, which can lead to N+1 if the relations are requested in a loop. To solve N+1, EAGER loading, JOIN FETCH, batch fetching or Entity Graphs are used.","What LAZY loading and the N+1 problem are\\nLAZY loading is the strategy in which related data is loaded from the database only at the moment it is first accessed. The N+1 problem arises when an application carries out one query to obtain a list of entities (10 users, for example), and then for every user carries out a separate query to load the related data (their orders, for example). The result is 1 + N queries instead of one.\\nWhy LAZY does not solve the problem\\nLAZY loading does not in itself prevent N+1; on the contrary, it can provoke it. If a developer accesses a lazy relation inside a loop, every call triggers a new SQL query. For example:\\n\\nHere LAZY loading leads to N+1. To avoid that, you have to tell the ORM explicitly to load the relations in advance.\\nHow do you solve the N+1 problem?\\n\\n- EAGER loading — loads the relations at once, but it can lead to superfluous data and a reduction in performance.\\n- JOIN FETCH — an explicit query with the joining of tables, for example: \`SELECT u FROM User u JOIN FETCH u.orders\`.\\n- Batch fetching — the loading of the relations in batches (of 10 entities at a time, for example).\\n- Entity Graphs — the dynamic statement of which relations to load for a specific query.\\nConclusion\\nLAZY loading is a tool of optimisation, but it is not a panacea against N+1. It should be combined with other techniques, such as JOIN FETCH or batch fetching, in order to avoid superfluous queries. The correct choice of loading strategy depends on the context: for lists with a large number of records it is better to use explicit queries with the preloading of the relations.","List<User> users = userRepository.findAll(); // 1 query\\nfor (User user : users) {\\n    System.out.println(user.getOrders().size()); // N queries\\n}"],[871,"Join fetch is an instruction in JPQL/HQL that forces Hibernate to load the related entities in a single SQL query by means of a JOIN. That solves the problem of N+1 queries, where a separate query to the database is carried out for every entity. When loading a list of orders with their items, for example, join fetch lets you obtain all of the data in one query.","What is join fetch?\\nJoin fetch is a mechanism in JPA/Hibernate that lets you load related entities (collections or references, for example) in a single SQL query by means of a JOIN. It is used in JPQL/HQL queries to state explicitly that an association should be loaded eagerly, even if it is configured as lazy.\\nHow does it work?\\nWhen you write a query with join fetch, Hibernate generates SQL with a LEFT JOIN or an INNER JOIN, which extracts the data from several tables at once. That prevents the problem of N+1 queries, where for every parent entity a separate query is carried out to load the related data.\\nAn example of code\\nLet us look at the entities Order and OrderItem:\\n\\nWithout join fetch, loading all of the orders with their items will lead to N+1 queries:\\n\\nWith join fetch all of the data is loaded in one query:\\n\\nImportant nuances\\n\\n- Join fetch works only for queries, not for find() methods.\\n- When using join fetch with collections, the duplication of rows can arise (because of the JOIN), which is solved through DISTINCT.\\n- You cannot use join fetch for more than one collection in a single query — that will lead to a MultipleBagFetchException.\\nConclusion\\nJoin fetch is a powerful tool for optimising performance when working with JPA/Hibernate. It is worth applying in cases where you know for certain that you will need the related data, in order to avoid the problem of N+1 queries and reduce the load on the database.","@Entity\\npublic class Order {\\n    @Id\\n    private Long id;\\n    @OneToMany(mappedBy = \\"order\\", fetch = FetchType.LAZY)\\n    private List<OrderItem> items;\\n}\\n\\n@Entity\\npublic class OrderItem {\\n    @Id\\n    private Long id;\\n    @ManyToOne\\n    private Order order;\\n}\\n\\n// Bad: 1 query for the orders + N queries for the items\\nList<Order> orders = em.createQuery(\\"SELECT o FROM Order o\\", Order.class).getResultList();\\nfor (Order o : orders) {\\n    System.out.println(o.getItems().size()); // every call raises SQL\\n}\\n\\n// Good: one SQL query with a JOIN\\nList<Order> orders = em.createQuery(\\"SELECT o FROM Order o JOIN FETCH o.items\\", Order.class).getResultList();\\nfor (Order o : orders) {\\n    System.out.println(o.getItems().size()); // the data has already been loaded\\n}"],[872,"To manage URLs in different environments, environment variables or configuration files are used. In React that is done through .env files with the REACT_APP_ prefix. In Node.js you can use the dotenv module. On the server the variables are set in the system or in CI/CD. That lets you change the URL without changing the code.","Managing the configuration of URLs for different environments\\nWhen developing web applications you often have to use different URLs for the API, databases or external services depending on the environment: development (dev), testing (test) and production (prod). Stating the URL directly in the code leads to problems during deployment and increases the risk of errors. Instead, environment variables or configuration files are applied.\\nThe use of environment variables\\nThe most widespread approach is to hold the URLs in environment variables. In Node.js that is done by means of the \`dotenv\` module, which loads the variables from a \`.env\` file. For different environments separate files are created: \`.env.development\`, \`.env.test\`, \`.env.production\`. In React applications the variables have to begin with \`REACT_APP_\`.\\n\\nIn the code the access happens through \`process.env.REACT_APP_API_URL\`. During the build the file needed is substituted automatically depending on the command.\\nConfiguration files\\nThe alternative method is to create configuration files for every environment — \`config.dev.js\`, \`config.prod.js\`, for example. Depending on the \`NODE_ENV\` variable the file needed is loaded. This approach is convenient when you have to manage not only the URLs but also other settings.\\n\\nApplication in CI/CD\\nIn continuous integration (CI/CD) systems the environment variables are set at the level of the pipeline or the server. That lets you substitute the URL for every environment dynamically without changing the code. In a Docker container, for example, the variables are passed through \`docker run -e\` or a \`.env\` file.\\nConclusion\\nThe use of environment variables or configuration files to manage URLs is standard practice, which makes an application flexible, secure and easily deployable in different environments. It is recommended to apply this approach in every project where there are several environments.","// .env.development\\nREACT_APP_API_URL=http://localhost:3000/api\\n\\n// .env.production\\nREACT_APP_API_URL=https://api.example.com\\n\\n// config.js\\nconst config = {\\n  development: { apiUrl: 'http://localhost:3000/api' },\\n  production: { apiUrl: 'https://api.example.com' }\\n};\\n\\nconst env = process.env.NODE_ENV || 'development';\\nmodule.exports = config[env];"],[873,"The printStackTrace() method prints the call stack to the standard error stream, which is often not saved in the logs. In production that leads to the loss of information about the error and makes monitoring and diagnosis more difficult. Instead you should use logging libraries such as Log4j or SLF4J, which let you configure the output, the rotation and the logging levels flexibly.","Why is printStackTrace() dangerous in production?\\nThe \`printStackTrace()\` method of the \`Throwable\` class prints the stack trace of an exception to the standard error stream (System.err). In local development that is convenient for fast debugging, but in an industrial environment such an approach creates serious problems.\\nThe main shortcomings\\n\\n- The loss of information: The standard error stream is often not redirected into log files, especially in containerised environments (Docker, Kubernetes). If the container restarts, every message is lost.\\n- The absence of context: You cannot add additional information to the error, such as the ID of the request, the name of the user or a timestamp in the format needed.\\n- The difficulty of monitoring: Log-aggregation systems (ELK, Splunk) expect structured data. printStackTrace() prints unstructured text, which is hard to parse.\\n- Problems of performance: In highly loaded systems the frequent calling of printStackTrace() can create an excessive load on the input-output.\\nThe correct approach\\nUse logging libraries such as SLF4J with Logback or Log4j2. They provide:\\n\\n- Logging levels (ERROR, WARN, INFO, DEBUG)\\n- Output formats with timestamps\\n- The rotation of files by size or by time\\n- The ability to send the logs to centralised systems\\nAn example of code\\n\\nIn this example SLF4J will automatically add the stack trace to the log, but it will at the same time preserve the structure and let you configure the output.\\nConclusion\\nUse printStackTrace() only for fast debugging in a local environment. In production always apply professional logging libraries — that will ensure the preservation of the data, make monitoring simpler and raise the reliability of the application.","import org.slf4j.Logger;\\nimport org.slf4j.LoggerFactory;\\n\\npublic class UserService {\\n    private static final Logger logger = LoggerFactory.getLogger(UserService.class);\\n\\n    public User getUserById(Long id) {\\n        try {\\n            // the business logic\\n        } catch (Exception e) {\\n            // BAD: printStackTrace()\\n            // e.printStackTrace();\\n\\n            // GOOD: logging with context\\n            logger.error(\\"Failed to fetch user with id: {}\\", id, e);\\n        }\\n    }\\n}"],[874,"REST (Representational State Transfer) is an architectural style for building web services, where the resources (the data) are represented in the form of URLs and the operations on them are carried out by means of HTTP methods. CRUD is an abbreviation of four basic operations: Create, Read, Update, Delete. In REST these operations usually correspond to the HTTP methods: POST (Create), GET (Read), PUT/PATCH (Update), DELETE (Delete). RESTful APIs use these methods to work with resources, which makes them simple and predictable.","What is REST?\\nREST (Representational State Transfer) is an architectural style proposed by Roy Fielding in 2000. It describes how a client and a server can exchange data over HTTP. The main idea of REST is work with resources. A resource is any object, data or service that can be accessed by a unique URL (/users/123, for example).\\nWhat is CRUD?\\nCRUD is an acronym for the four main operations that can be carried out with data: Create, Read, Update, Delete. In the context of a RESTful API these operations map onto the HTTP methods:\\n\\n- POST — Create (create a new resource)\\n- GET — Read (obtain a resource or a list of resources)\\n- PUT — Update (replace a resource entirely)\\n- PATCH — Update (update a resource partially)\\n- DELETE — Delete (delete a resource)\\nAn example of a RESTful API for managing users\\nSuppose we have the resource \\"users\\". Here is how the CRUD operations will look:\\n\\nThe principles of REST\\n\\n- Stateless: every request from the client to the server has to contain all of the information necessary for handling it. The server does not hold the client's state between requests.\\n- Caching: the server's responses can be marked as cacheable or non-cacheable, in order to improve performance.\\n- The uniformity of the interface: the use of standard HTTP methods and status codes (200 OK, 201 Created, 404 Not Found, 500 Internal Server Error).\\n- A layered system: the client does not know whether it is communicating with the server directly or through intermediate nodes (proxies, balancers).\\nConclusion\\nREST and CRUD are fundamental concepts for building modern web APIs. REST provides the architectural principles, while CRUD provides the specific operations for managing data. The use of a RESTful API makes a system simple, scalable and easily integrable with various clients (web, mobile applications).","// Create a user (Create)\\nPOST /users\\nBody: { \\"name\\": \\"John\\", \\"email\\": \\"john@example.com\\" }\\n\\n// Obtain every user (Read)\\nGET /users\\n\\n// Obtain the user with id=1 (Read)\\nGET /users/1\\n\\n// Replace the user with id=1 entirely (Update)\\nPUT /users/1\\nBody: { \\"name\\": \\"Jane\\", \\"email\\": \\"jane@example.com\\" }\\n\\n// Update the email of the user with id=1 partially (Update)\\nPATCH /users/1\\nBody: { \\"email\\": \\"newemail@example.com\\" }\\n\\n// Delete the user with id=1 (Delete)\\nDELETE /users/1"],[875,"ConcurrentHashMap does not lock the whole map in its entirety. Instead it uses the technique of division into segments (lock striping), where every segment is locked independently. That lets several threads read and write data in different segments at the same time, which substantially raises performance.","How ConcurrentHashMap works\\nConcurrentHashMap is a thread-safe implementation of HashMap in Java, which uses the technique of division into segments (lock striping). Instead of locking the whole map, it divides the internal structure into several segments, each of which has its own lock. That lets several threads carry out operations on different segments at the same time.\\nAn example of code\\n\\nThe advantages\\n\\n- High performance during concurrent access\\n- The absence of a lock for read operations (in Java 8+ implemented through CAS)\\n- Scalability as the number of threads increases\\nConclusion\\nConcurrentHashMap should be used in multithreaded applications where high performance and thread safety are required, especially with frequent read and write operations from different threads.","ConcurrentHashMap<String, Integer> map = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();\\n\\n// Thread 1\\nmap.put(\\"key1\\", 1);\\n\\n// Thread 2\\nmap.put(\\"key2\\", 2);\\n\\n// Both threads can work at the same time, since the keys fall into different segments"],[876,"A greedy algorithm is an approach in which at every stage the best decision at that moment is taken, without taking the future consequences into account. It is simple to implement and is often used for tasks where the local choice leads to the global optimum — in Dijkstra's algorithm or the coin-change problem, for example. It does not always guarantee an optimal solution, however, so it does not suit every task.","What is a greedy algorithm?\\nA greedy algorithm is a strategy for solving tasks in which at every step the locally optimal solution is chosen, in the hope that this will lead to a globally optimal result. It does not review its previous decisions and does not consider alternatives, which makes it fast and simple to implement.\\nHow does it work?\\nThe algorithm takes decisions sequentially, basing them only on the current information. In the coin-change problem for the sum 11 with the coins 1, 2, 5, for example, the greedy algorithm will choose 5, then 5, then 1 — 3 coins in all, which is optimal. For the coins 1, 3, 4 and the sum 6, however, it will choose 4, 1, 1 (3 coins), although 3 + 3 (2 coins) is optimal.\\nAn example of code in Python\\n\\nWhere is it applied?\\n\\n- Dijkstra's algorithm for finding the shortest path in a graph.\\n- The knapsack problem (the fractional version).\\n- The building of a minimum spanning tree (Prim's and Kruskal's algorithms).\\n- Huffman coding for the compression of data.\\nConclusion\\nGreedy algorithms are effective for tasks where the local optimum coincides with the global one — in tasks with an optimal substructure, for example. They are worth applying when speed and simplicity matter, but it is necessary to be sure the task permits such a solution.","def greedy_coin_change(coins, amount):\\n    coins.sort(reverse=True)\\n    result = []\\n    for coin in coins:\\n        while amount >= coin:\\n            amount -= coin\\n            result.append(coin)\\n    return result\\n\\nprint(greedy_coin_change([1, 2, 5], 11))  # [5, 5, 1]"],[877,"Big-O describes the rate of growth of a function rather than the exact execution time. Constants such as 2N do not affect the asymptotic behaviour at large N, so they are discarded. O(2N), for example, simplifies to O(N), since both grow linearly. That lets you compare algorithms by their scalability, ignoring hardware or language peculiarities.","Why constants are ignored in Big-O\\nBig-O notation is used to describe the asymptotic complexity of an algorithm — that is, how the execution time or the use of memory grows as the size of the input data (N) increases. Constant multipliers, such as the 2 in 2N, do not affect the overall form of the growth — linear growth stays linear, regardless of whether N is multiplied by 2 or by 100. Big-O therefore simplifies the expression to O(N).\\nAn example for understanding\\nLet us look at two algorithms: the first carries out 2N operations, the second 100N operations. Both have linear complexity O(N). At N = 1,000,000 a difference of 50 times can be significant, but Big-O shows that both algorithms scale equally badly at enormous N. If one algorithm has the complexity O(N) and the other O(N^2), however, then at large N the quadratic growth will become dominant, and the constants are already unimportant.\\nA practical example of code\\n\\nConclusion\\nConstants are ignored in Big-O because the notation focuses on the asymptotic behaviour at large N. That lets developers compare algorithms by their scalability without being distracted by the details of the implementation or by hardware differences. In practice, however, the constants can matter for the choice between algorithms with the same asymptotics, especially at small N.","// Algorithm 1: O(N) — a single pass over the array\\nfunction findMax(arr) {\\n  let max = arr[0];\\n  for (let i = 1; i < arr.length; i++) {\\n    if (arr[i] > max) max = arr[i];\\n  }\\n  return max;\\n}\\n\\n// Algorithm 2: O(2N) — two passes (the constant 2)\\nfunction findMaxTwice(arr) {\\n  let max = arr[0];\\n  for (let i = 1; i < arr.length; i++) {\\n    if (arr[i] > max) max = arr[i];\\n  }\\n  // The second pass (superfluous)\\n  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {\\n    if (arr[i] > max) max = arr[i];\\n  }\\n  return max;\\n}\\n// Both algorithms are O(N), although the second does twice as many operations."],[878,"Events in JavaScript pass through three phases: capturing, the target phase and bubbling. In the capturing phase the event first goes from top to bottom down the DOM tree, beginning with the parent element and reaching the target element. Then the event is handled on the target element, after which it rises back up the tree (the bubbling phase). That lets you assign handlers at different levels and use event delegation.","Events in JavaScript pass through three phases, which help determine how they are handled on a web page:\\n\\n- The capturing phase: In this phase the event is \\"captured\\" from top to bottom down the DOM tree. First it reaches the top level of the elements and moves down to the target element. In this phase the event handlers set on the parent elements can react to the event. For a handler to fire in the capturing phase, the third argument has to be stated as \`true\` when the event handler is added.\\n\\n- The target phase: The event reaches the target element on which it happened. The event handlers attached directly to that element are carried out in this phase. If the target element has event handlers, they will be called in this phase.\\n\\n- The bubbling phase: After the event has been handled on the target element, it starts to \\"bubble\\" back up the DOM tree, passing from the child element to the parent. The event handlers set on the parent elements can be called in this phase. That is useful for event delegation, since you can set a single handler on the parent element and react to the events of the child elements.\\n\\nAn understanding of the phases of events lets developers manage the handling of events on web pages more effectively and use techniques such as event delegation to optimise code and raise performance.","document.getElementById('parent').addEventListener('click', function() {\\n  console.log('The capturing of the event!'); \\n}, true);\\n\\ndocument.getElementById('myButton').addEventListener('click', function() {\\n  console.log('The button has been pressed!'); \\n});\\n\\ndocument.getElementById('parent').addEventListener('click', function() {\\n  console.log('The bubbling of the event!'); \\n});"],[879,"Big-O describes how quickly the execution time or the use of memory of an algorithm grows as the input data increases. For example, O(1) is constant time, O(n) linear, O(n²) quadratic. That helps you compare algorithms and choose the optimal one for large data.","What is Big-O?\\nBig-O is a mathematical notation that describes the upper bound of the growth of an algorithm's complexity function. It shows how an algorithm will behave as the size of the input data tends to infinity. The main attention is given to the fastest-growing term, ignoring the constants and the smaller addends.\\nThe main classes of complexity\\n\\n- O(1) — constant complexity: the time does not depend on the size of the data (access to an element of an array by index, for example).\\n- O(log n) — logarithmic: the time grows slowly (a binary search in a sorted array, for example).\\n- O(n) — linear: the time is proportional to the size of the data (the search for an element in an unsorted list, for example).\\n- O(n log n) — often met in effective sorts (quicksort, merge sort, for example).\\n- O(n²) — quadratic: the time grows quadratically (bubble sort, for example).\\n- O(2ⁿ) — exponential: it grows very quickly (the recursive computation of Fibonacci numbers without memoisation, for example).\\nAn example of code\\n\\nHow do you apply Big-O?\\nWhen choosing an algorithm for a task it matters to take the expected size of the input data into account. For small arrays the difference between O(n²) and O(n log n) may be unnoticeable, but for millions of elements it becomes critical. Big-O helps you predict performance and avoid bottlenecks.\\nConclusion: Use Big-O to analyse and compare algorithms, especially when working with large volumes of data. It is a fundamental tool for writing scalable, effective code.","// O(1) — constant complexity\\nfunction getFirstElement(arr) {\\n  return arr[0];\\n}\\n\\n// O(n) — linear complexity\\nfunction findElement(arr, target) {\\n  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {\\n    if (arr[i] === target) return i;\\n  }\\n  return -1;\\n}\\n\\n// O(n²) — quadratic complexity\\nfunction bubbleSort(arr) {\\n  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {\\n    for (let j = 0; j < arr.length - i - 1; j++) {\\n      if (arr[j] > arr[j + 1]) {\\n        [arr[j], arr[j + 1]] = [arr[j + 1], arr[j]];\\n      }\\n    }\\n  }\\n  return arr;\\n}"],[880,"DIP states that top-level modules should not depend on lower-level modules. Both should depend on abstractions. Abstractions should not depend on details, and the details should depend on abstractions. That lets you easily replace implementations and test the code.","What is the Dependency Inversion Principle?\\nThe Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP) is the fifth principle of SOLID, which is aimed at reducing the coupling between modules. It proposes building the architecture so that the high-level modules (the business logic) do not depend on the low-level ones (databases, APIs). Instead, both levels should depend on abstractions (interfaces).\\nHow does it work?\\nInstead of a class creating an instance of another class directly, it receives it through the constructor or a method (dependency injection). That lets you easily substitute implementations — for testing, for example.\\nAn example of code\\n\\nWhere is it applied?\\n\\n- Dependency injection (DI) in frameworks (Spring, Nest.js, Angular).\\n- Testing: it is easy to substitute a mock for the real service.\\n- Scaling: the replacement of one implementation with another without changing the code.\\nConclusion\\nDIP helps you build flexible, testable and maintainable code. Apply it in any project where modularity and the ability to replace components matter.","// Bad: the high-level module depends on a specific implementation\\nclass EmailService {\\n  send(message) { /* the sending of the email */ }\\n}\\nclass Notification {\\n  constructor() {\\n    this.service = new EmailService(); // a rigid coupling\\n  }\\n  notify(msg) { this.service.send(msg); }\\n}\\n\\n// Good: a dependence on an abstraction\\nclass Notification {\\n  constructor(service) { // dependency injection\\n    this.service = service;\\n  }\\n  notify(msg) { this.service.send(msg); }\\n}\\n// Now any service implementing the send interface can be passed"],[881,"Dependency Inversion (DIP) states that top-level modules should not depend on lower-level modules; both should depend on abstractions. It is implemented through interfaces: the top-level class uses an interface, and the specific implementation is substituted from outside. That reduces the coupling and makes testing and the replacement of components simpler.","What is Dependency Inversion?\\nThe Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP) is the fifth principle of SOLID. It asserts that top-level modules should not depend on lower-level modules; both should depend on abstractions. Abstractions should not depend on details; the details should depend on abstractions. In practice that means that instead of a class creating instances of its dependencies directly, it receives them through an interface.\\nThe implementation through interfaces\\nAn interface defines the contract (the set of methods) that the specific dependency has to implement. The top-level class works only with that interface, knowing nothing about the specific implementation. That lets you easily replace one implementation with another (for testing or for a change of database, for example).\\nAn example of code\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\n\\n- Dependency injection (DI) in frameworks (Spring, ASP.NET Core, NestJS).\\n- Testing: the substitution of mocks for the real services.\\n- Modular systems, where the components can be replaced without changing the code.\\nConclusion: Dependency Inversion through interfaces makes code flexible, testable and easily extensible. Apply it everywhere the replacement of an implementation is possible, especially in large projects with a modular architecture.","// The interface of the abstraction\\ninterface ILogger {\\n    void Log(string message);\\n}\\n\\n// The specific implementation\\nclass ConsoleLogger : ILogger {\\n    public void Log(string message) {\\n        Console.WriteLine(message);\\n    }\\n}\\n\\n// The top-level class depends on the abstraction\\nclass OrderService {\\n    private readonly ILogger _logger;\\n    \\n    public OrderService(ILogger logger) {\\n        _logger = logger;\\n    }\\n    \\n    public void CreateOrder() {\\n        // the business logic\\n        _logger.Log(\\"Order created\\");\\n    }\\n}\\n\\n// The injection of the dependency through the constructor\\nvar logger = new ConsoleLogger();\\nvar service = new OrderService(logger);"],[882,"With concurrent access, race conditions arise, where the result depends on the order in which the threads are carried out. Deadlocks are also possible, where the threads wait for one another. Other problems: livelock, starvation and priority inversion. To solve them, synchronisation through mutexes, semaphores or locks is used.","The main problems of concurrent access\\nWhen several threads or processes access shared data at the same time, typical problems arise, connected with the absence of control over the order of execution. The best-known is the race condition: the result of an operation depends on which thread is carried out first. Two threads increase a counter at the same time, for example: each reads the value, adds one and writes it back. If they cross, one of the increments will be lost.\\nDeadlocks and other problems\\n\\n- Deadlock — two or more threads wait for resources held by one another, and none can continue.\\n- Livelock — the threads constantly change their state in response to one another's actions, but there is no progress.\\n- Starvation — one thread does not gain access to a resource, because the others constantly take it first.\\n- Priority inversion — a low-priority thread blocks a high-priority one by holding on to the resource needed.\\nAn example of code with a race condition\\n\\nHow to solve the problems\\nMechanisms of synchronisation are used: mutexes, semaphores, critical sections. In Python, for example, threading.Lock; in Java, synchronized; in Go, channels. It matters to design the code so as to minimise the shared resources and to use atomic operations.\\nConclusion: An understanding of the problems of concurrent access is necessary when developing multithreaded applications, servers, databases and any systems where the integrity of the data matters. The application of the right mechanisms of synchronisation prevents elusive bugs.","let counter = 0;\\n\\nfunction increment() {\\n  let temp = counter;  // the read\\n  temp = temp + 1;     // the change\\n  counter = temp;      // the write\\n}\\n\\n// Two threads call increment() at the same time\\n// A possible result: counter = 1 instead of 2"],[883,"Concurrent access is the situation where several threads or processes read and write the same data at the same time. That can lead to a race condition, where the result depends on the order in which the threads are carried out. To prevent problems, mechanisms of synchronisation such as mutexes, semaphores and locks are used.","What is concurrent access?\\nConcurrent access to data arises when several threads of execution or processes access a shared resource at the same time — a variable, a file or a record in a database, for example. If at least one of the threads changes the data while the others read it or also change it, a race condition can arise. That leads to unpredictable results, to errors that are hard to reproduce and to the program crashing.\\nAn example of the problem\\nLet us look at a simple counter that is increased by two threads:\\n\\nThat is the classic example of a race condition. The read-modify-write operation is not atomic, and without synchronisation the threads can overwrite one another's results.\\nHow do you solve it?\\nTo protect shared data, primitives of synchronisation are used:\\n\\n- A Mutex — blocks access to the resource for the other threads while one thread works with it.\\n- A semaphore — limits the number of threads that can gain access to the resource at the same time.\\n- Atomic operations — indivisible operations that are carried out in a single step (an atomic increment, for example).\\nAn example with a mutex in Python:\\n\\nWhere is it applied?\\nConcurrent access is relevant in multithreaded servers, databases (transactions), operating systems and web applications with shared memory. An understanding of this concept is critical for writing reliable, scalable code.\\nConclusion: Concurrent access requires mandatory synchronisation in order to prevent a race condition. Use mutexes, semaphores or atomic operations depending on the language and the task. It is the foundation for building correct multithreaded systems.","counter = 0\\n\\ndef increment():\\n    global counter\\n    temp = counter\\n    temp += 1\\n    counter = temp\\n\\n# Thread 1 and Thread 2 carry out increment() at the same time\\n# A possible scenario:\\n# Thread 1: temp = 0\\n# Thread 2: temp = 0\\n# Thread 1: temp = 1, counter = 1\\n# Thread 2: temp = 1, counter = 1\\n# counter = 2 was expected, but we got 1\\n\\nimport threading\\n\\ncounter = 0\\nlock = threading.Lock()\\n\\ndef safe_increment():\\n    global counter\\n    with lock:\\n        temp = counter\\n        temp += 1\\n        counter = temp\\n\\n# Now the threads are carried out sequentially inside the with block"],[884,"Polymorphism is the ability of objects with the same interface to behave differently depending on their type. Abstraction is the hiding of the complex details of an implementation and the provision of only the interface necessary. More simply, abstraction answers the question \\"what does the object do\\", while polymorphism answers \\"how exactly does it do it\\".","The main difference\\nAbstraction and polymorphism are two different but mutually complementary principles of object-oriented programming. Abstraction lets you hide the complexity of an implementation by providing the user with only the interface necessary. Polymorphism, for its part, lets a single interface work with different data types or objects.\\nAbstraction in practice\\nImagine you are using a television remote control. You press the \\"on\\" button, and the television comes on. You do not have to know how exactly the power circuit works or how the signal is passed to the screen. That is abstraction — you work with a simple interface without going into the complex details.\\n\\nPolymorphism in action\\nPolymorphism lets you use objects of different classes through a single interface. Continuing the example with animals, we can call the \`make_sound()\` method for any object that inherits from \`Animal\`, and every object will carry out its own version of the method.\\n\\nThe key differences\\n\\n- The aim: Abstraction hides complexity, polymorphism provides flexibility.\\n- The implementation: Abstraction is achieved through abstract classes and interfaces, polymorphism through the overriding and overloading of methods.\\n- The focus: Abstraction focuses on what the object does, polymorphism on how it does it.\\nConclusion: Abstraction and polymorphism work together to build flexible, maintainable code. Abstraction defines the contract (the interface), while polymorphism lets different classes implement that contract in their own way. That is especially useful when building frameworks and libraries and when working with collections of heterogeneous objects.","// An example of abstraction in Python\\nfrom abc import ABC, abstractmethod\\n\\nclass Animal(ABC):\\n    @abstractmethod\\n    def make_sound(self):\\n        pass\\n\\nclass Dog(Animal):\\n    def make_sound(self):\\n        return \\"Woof\\"\\n\\nclass Cat(Animal):\\n    def make_sound(self):\\n        return \\"Meow\\"\\n\\n# The use of polymorphism\\ndef animal_sound(animal):\\n    print(animal.make_sound())\\n\\ndog = Dog()\\ncat = Cat()\\n\\nanimal_sound(dog)  # Will print: Woof\\nanimal_sound(cat)  # Will print: Meow"],[885,"Access modifiers control the visibility of a class's fields and methods. Private makes a member of a class accessible only inside the class itself, hiding the details of the implementation. Protected opens access for the class and its descendants, letting the behaviour be overridden. It is the foundation of encapsulation, protecting data from incorrect use.","The purpose of access modifiers\\nIn object-oriented programming the access modifiers (private, protected, public) determine which parts of the code can access a class's fields and methods. They implement the principle of encapsulation — the hiding of an object's internal state and the provision of a controlled interface.\\nPrivate\\nA field or a method with the private modifier is accessible only inside the same class. That lets you hide the details of the implementation that should not be changed from outside — an internal counter or temporary data, for example.\\n\\nHere balance is private, so outside code cannot change it directly, only through the deposit and getBalance methods.\\nProtected\\nProtected extends the access: the members of the class are visible inside the class itself and in descendant classes. That is convenient when you have to permit subclasses to use or override the behaviour while hiding it from the outside world.\\n\\nThe makeSound method is accessible in Dog, but not from an instance of Animal or from outside code.\\nConclusion\\nPrivate and protected are key tools for building a reliable architecture. Private protects the internal data from accidental change, while protected lets you extend classes flexibly through inheritance while preserving control over the interface. Their use is mandatory in any serious project in order to support encapsulation and reduce the coupling of the code.","class BankAccount {\\n  private balance: number;\\n\\n  constructor(initial: number) {\\n    this.balance = initial;\\n  }\\n\\n  public deposit(amount: number): void {\\n    if (amount > 0) {\\n      this.balance += amount;\\n    }\\n  }\\n\\n  public getBalance(): number {\\n    return this.balance;\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nclass Animal {\\n  protected name: string;\\n\\n  constructor(name: string) {\\n    this.name = name;\\n  }\\n\\n  protected makeSound(): void {\\n    console.log('Some sound');\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nclass Dog extends Animal {\\n  public bark(): void {\\n    this.makeSound(); // accessible, since it is protected\\n    console.log('Woof!');\\n  }\\n}"],[886,"The main principles of OOP: encapsulation, inheritance, polymorphism and abstraction. Encapsulation hides an object's internal details. Inheritance lets you create classes on the basis of others. Polymorphism gives you the ability to work with different types through a single interface. Abstraction singles out only the important characteristics.","Object-oriented programming (OOP) is built on four key principles, which help you build flexible, reusable and maintainable code. These principles — encapsulation, inheritance, polymorphism and abstraction — are the foundation for designing classes and objects.\\nEncapsulation\\nThis is the mechanism that hides an object's internal state from the outside world and provides access to it only through public methods. That protects the data from incorrect change and makes maintaining the code simpler.\\n\\nInheritance\\nLets you create a new class on the basis of an existing one, borrowing its properties and methods. That reduces the duplication of code and builds a hierarchy.\\n\\nPolymorphism\\nGives you the ability to use objects of different classes through a single interface. The \`speak()\` method, for example, can work differently for a cat and a dog, but be called in the same way.\\n\\nAbstraction\\nThis is the principle by which we hide the complex details of an implementation and show only the functions necessary. When working with a car, for example, we use the steering wheel and the pedals without going into the working of the engine.\\n\\nConclusion: a knowledge of these principles is necessary for designing clean, scalable and easily maintainable systems. They are applied in most modern languages (Java, C++, Python, JavaScript) and help developers speak the same language when discussing architecture.","class BankAccount {\\n  #balance = 0; // a private field\\n\\n  deposit(amount) {\\n    if (amount > 0) this.#balance += amount;\\n  }\\n\\n  getBalance() {\\n    return this.#balance;\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nclass Animal {\\n  constructor(name) { this.name = name; }\\n  speak() { console.log(\`\${this.name} makes a sound\`); }\\n}\\n\\nclass Dog extends Animal {\\n  speak() { console.log(\`\${this.name} barks\`); }\\n}\\n\\nconst animals = [new Dog('Rex'), new Cat('Whiskers')];\\nanimals.forEach(a => a.speak()); // every object reacts in its own way\\n\\nclass CoffeeMachine {\\n  #boilWater() { /* a complex process */ }\\n  makeCoffee() {\\n    this.#boilWater();\\n    // a simple interface for the user\\n  }\\n}"],[887,"The testing of backend/data processes focuses on checking APIs, business logic, the handling of data and integrations, often without a graphical interface. Web applications are tested at the level of the UI, UX, cross-browser compatibility and the performance of the interface. Backend tests are usually automated and use unit, integration and load tests, whereas web tests include functional, regression and visual testing.","The main differences in the approaches to testing\\nThe testing of backend and data processes differs from the testing of web applications in several key aspects. Backend testing is concentrated on checking the server logic, the APIs, the databases and the handling of data, whereas web testing includes checking the user interface, the interaction with the browser and the visual display.\\nThe objects of testing\\n\\n- Backend/data processes: API endpoints, business logic, transactions, ETL processes, the integrity of the data, the server's performance.\\n- Web applications: UI elements, navigation, forms, adaptiveness, cross-browser compatibility, accessibility.\\nThe tools and methods\\nFor backend testing, unit tests (with pytest for Python, for example), integration tests (checking the interaction with a database or with external services) and load tests (by means of JMeter or Locust) are often used. Web testing includes functional tests (Selenium, Cypress), regression tests and visual testing (Percy).\\nAn example of code for a backend test\\n\\nAn example of code for a web test\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe choice of approach depends on the aims: backend testing is critical for ensuring the reliability and the performance of the server part, while web testing is critical for the quality of the user experience. In modern projects both types of testing complement one another, ensuring complete coverage.","import pytest\\nfrom app import create_app, db\\n\\n@pytest.fixture\\ndef client():\\n    app = create_app()\\n    app.config['TESTING'] = True\\n    with app.test_client() as client:\\n        yield client\\n\\ndef test_get_user(client):\\n    response = client.get('/api/users/1')\\n    assert response.status_code == 200\\n    assert 'username' in response.json\\n\\nfrom selenium import webdriver\\n\\ndef test_login_page():\\n    driver = webdriver.Chrome()\\n    driver.get('https://example.com/login')\\n    driver.find_element_by_id('username').send_keys('testuser')\\n    driver.find_element_by_id('password').send_keys('password123')\\n    driver.find_element_by_id('submit').click()\\n    assert 'Dashboard' in driver.title\\n    driver.quit()"],[888,"The browser cache can save pages that require authorisation and show them even after the user has logged out. That can lead to another user seeing confidential data. To prevent it, the headers Cache-Control: no-store, no-cache and Pragma: no-cache are used, which forbid the caching of protected pages.","The influence of the browser cache on authorisation\\nThe browser cache is intended to speed up the loading of pages by saving copies of resources. When working with authorised pages, however, that can create serious problems of security. If a page containing a user's personal data is cached, then after they log out another user opening the same page can see the cached version with the data of the previous session.\\nHow it works\\nWhen a user authorises and views protected pages, the browser can save them in the cache. If the server does not send the corresponding headers, the browser will use the cached version on a repeated request, even if the session has already ended. That is especially dangerous on publicly accessible computers.\\nAn example of the solution\\nTo prevent the caching of protected pages, the server has to send the following HTTP headers:\\n\\nAn example in Node.js with Express:\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe use of the correct caching headers is critically important for the security of authorised pages. It prevents the leak of data and protects users when working on shared devices.","Cache-Control: no-store, no-cache, must-revalidate, proxy-revalidate\\nPragma: no-cache\\nExpires: 0\\n\\napp.get('/dashboard', (req, res) => {\\n  res.set('Cache-Control', 'no-store, no-cache, must-revalidate');\\n  res.set('Pragma', 'no-cache');\\n  res.set('Expires', '0');\\n  // the serving of the protected content\\n});"],[889,"To cancel the default action when an event arises in JavaScript, the \`event.preventDefault()\` method is used. That lets you block the standard behaviour of elements, such as following a link or submitting a form. If you do not want following a link to happen when it is clicked, for example, you can cancel that action.","The \`event.preventDefault()\` method is used to cancel the default action that is supposed to happen in response to an event. That is useful when you want to change the standard behaviour of elements or add your own logic. Clicking a link, for example, usually leads to moving to a new page, while submitting a form causes the page to reload.\\nAn example of the use of \`preventDefault()\`:\\n\\nIn this example, when the link is clicked, the move to another page will not happen, and instead the code inside the event handler will be carried out. That is especially useful when working with forms, when a check has to be carried out before the data is sent, or when creating interactive elements such as drop-down menus or modal windows.\\nCancelling the default action lets you manage the behaviour of the elements on a page flexibly, making web applications more interactive and convenient.","document.querySelector('a').addEventListener('click', function(event) {\\n  event.preventDefault();    \\n  console.log('Following the link has been cancelled.'); \\n});"],[890,"Errors of authorisation arise when a user has no rights to the action requested. The causes include incorrect or expired tokens, incorrect roles, the absence of the permissions necessary or an incorrect configuration of the server. The problem can also be a mismatch of the data in the request and on the server.","The main causes of errors of authorisation\\nErrors of authorisation (usually HTTP 403 Forbidden) arise when the server understands who you are but refuses access to the resource. That differs from errors of authentication (401 Unauthorized), where the identity has not been established.\\nThe widespread causes\\n\\n- An incorrect or expired token: a JWT can be invalid because of its expiry, an incorrect signature or a change of the payload.\\n- Insufficient rights of access: the user has been authenticated, but their role has no permission for the operation (an ordinary user tries to delete an administrative resource, for example).\\n- An incorrect configuration of the middleware: In Express or Nest.js the order of the middleware matters — if the authorisation check stands after the handler, it may not fire.\\n- The absence of headers: the client did not pass the Authorization header, or passed it in an incorrect format.\\n- Problems with CORS: Although CORS is not connected directly with authorisation, an incorrect configuration can block requests with tokens.\\nAn example of code in Node.js (Express)\\n\\nConclusion\\nErrors of authorisation require a check both of the client's side (the correctness of the token and the headers) and of the server's (the configuration of the middleware, the roles and the permissions). An understanding of these causes helps you diagnose and correct problems of access in applications quickly.","const express = require('express');\\nconst jwt = require('jsonwebtoken');\\n\\nconst app = express();\\n\\n// The middleware that checks the JWT\\nfunction authMiddleware(req, res, next) {\\n  const token = req.headers['authorization']?.split(' ')[1];\\n  if (!token) return res.status(401).json({ error: 'No token' });\\n\\n  try {\\n    const decoded = jwt.verify(token, 'secret');\\n    req.user = decoded;\\n    next();\\n  } catch (err) {\\n    return res.status(403).json({ error: 'Invalid or expired token' });\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// The protected route\\napp.get('/admin', authMiddleware, (req, res) => {\\n  if (req.user.role !== 'admin') {\\n    return res.status(403).json({ error: 'Insufficient permissions' });\\n  }\\n  res.json({ message: 'Welcome admin' });\\n});\\n\\napp.listen(3000);"],[891,"In Scrum testing is not a separate phase but is integrated into every sprint. The tester (QA) takes part in the planning, writes the test cases, carries out the testing during the development and after the tasks have finished. At the end of the sprint a demo and a retrospective are held, where the results of the testing are analysed.","Testing in Scrum: the integration into the sprint\\nIn Scrum testing is not set apart as a separate stage after the development but is an inseparable part of every sprint. That lets you reveal and correct defects quickly, ensuring the high quality of the product at every stage.\\nThe role of QA in the sprint\\nThe QA engineer takes an active part in every Scrum event:\\n\\n- Sprint planning: they assess the testing effort and help clarify the acceptance criteria (the Definition of Done).\\n- The daily stand-ups: they report on the progress of the testing and on the blockers.\\n- The development: they write the test cases and the automated tests in parallel with the development of the features.\\n- The testing: they carry out functional, regression and integration testing of the finished tasks.\\n- The sprint review (the demo): they demonstrate the results of the testing and confirm the readiness of the functionality.\\n- The retrospective: they propose improvements in the testing processes.\\nAn example of the process\\nLet us look at a simple example: the team is developing a registration form. At the beginning of the sprint QA, together with the developers, determines the acceptance criteria: the validation of the email, the checking of the password, the error messages. During the sprint the developer writes the code, while QA creates the test cases and the automated tests. Once the task has finished, QA carries out the testing, finds the bugs and enters them in the bug tracker. The developer corrects the bugs, and QA carries out the testing again. By the end of the sprint every task has to have been tested and to conform to the Definition of Done.\\nConclusion\\nThe integration of testing into Scrum lets you raise the quality of the product, shorten the time spent on correcting defects and ensure the transparency of the process for the whole team. This approach is especially useful in projects with frequent changes of requirements and short development cycles.",null],[892,"A hotfix is an urgent correction of a critical error in the production version of a product. It is created from the main/master branch, so as not to include unfinished changes from develop. Once the correction is done, the hotfix branch is merged back into main and develop. That lets you eliminate the problem quickly without delaying the release of new features.","What is a hotfix?\\nA hotfix is an emergency correction that is applied to the current production version of a software product in order to eliminate a critical error or vulnerability. Unlike ordinary features, a hotfix does not wait for the next planned release but is introduced as quickly as possible.\\nHow does a hotfix work in Git Flow?\\nIn the popular Git Flow branching model the hotfix branch is created from main/master. That guarantees that only the changes necessary to eliminate the problem get into the correction, without unfinished features from develop. Once the work has finished, the hotfix branch is merged back into main (with a tag of the new version) and into develop, so that the correction is not lost in future releases.\\nAn example of working with a hotfix\\n\\nWhen should a hotfix be applied?\\nA hotfix is used only for critical problems that block users' work or create a threat to security. It is not worth using a hotfix for small improvements or non-critical bugs — the ordinary development cycle through develop suits those.\\nConclusion: A hotfix is an important tool for reacting quickly to production incidents. It lets you isolate an urgent correction from the current development and minimise the risks during deployment.","# Create the hotfix branch from main\\ngit checkout main\\ngit checkout -b hotfix/1.2.1\\n\\n# Bring in the correction and commit it\\ngit add .\\ngit commit -m \\"Fix critical login bug\\"\\n\\n# Merge into main and develop\\ngit checkout main\\ngit merge --no-ff hotfix/1.2.1\\ngit tag -a v1.2.1 -m \\"Hotfix version 1.2.1\\"\\n\\ngit checkout develop\\ngit merge --no-ff hotfix/1.2.1\\n\\n# Delete the temporary branch\\ngit branch -d hotfix/1.2.1"],[893,"First check whether a response is arriving from the server. Open the Network tab in the browser's developer tools. If the request goes out but the response has an error, the problem is on the backend. If the response arrives but the interface works incorrectly, the problem is on the frontend. Also check the browser's console for JavaScript errors.","Localising the problem: frontend or backend\\nTo determine the source of an error it is necessary to check several levels of the interaction in sequence. Begin with an analysis of the network requests in the browser's developer tools (the Network tab). If the request is not sent, or is sent with incorrect data, the problem is on the frontend. If the request has been sent but the server returns an error code (4xx or 5xx), the problem is on the backend.\\nThe practical steps\\n\\n- Open the browser's console (F12) and the Network tab.\\n- Carry out the action that raises the error.\\n- Find the corresponding request in the list.\\n- Check the status of the response: 2xx — success, 4xx — a client error, 5xx — a server error.\\n- If the status is 2xx but the data is displayed incorrectly, the problem is on the frontend (the logic of handling the response).\\nAn example of code for the check\\n\\nAdditional signs\\n\\n- The error is reproduced only in one browser — more likely the frontend.\\n- The error affects every user — probably the backend.\\n- An error in the browser's console (a ReferenceError, a TypeError) — the frontend.\\n- An error in the server's logs — the backend.\\nConclusion: A systematic approach to the diagnosis, beginning with the network requests and ending with an analysis of the logs, lets you determine quickly at which level the problem arose and shorten the time spent on correcting it.","// Frontend: the sending of the request and the handling of the error\\nfetch('/api/data')\\n  .then(response => {\\n    if (!response.ok) {\\n      // A status of 4xx or 5xx — a problem on the backend\\n      console.error('Server error:', response.status);\\n    }\\n    return response.json();\\n  })\\n  .then(data => {\\n    // The data has been received but is displayed incorrectly — a problem on the frontend\\n    renderData(data);\\n  })\\n  .catch(err => {\\n    // A network error (no connection) — a problem on the frontend or the network\\n    console.error('Network error:', err);\\n  });"],[894,"The Network tab in DevTools is used to monitor every network request a page makes: the loading of HTML, CSS, JavaScript, images, API requests. It helps you analyse the loading time, the statuses of the responses and the sizes of the files, and reveal the bottlenecks. It is a key tool for optimising performance and debugging problems with the network.","The purpose of the Network tab\\nThe Network tab in the browser's developer tools (DevTools) is intended for tracking and analysing every network request a web page carries out. It lets a developer see which resources are being loaded, how long that takes and which errors arise. It is an indispensable tool for optimising performance, debugging API integrations and understanding an application's behaviour on the network.\\nThe main capabilities\\n\\n- Viewing the requests: it displays every request (XHR, Fetch, images, scripts, styles) in chronological order.\\n- The details of a request: on clicking a request you can see the Headers, the Response, the Payload, the Timing and the cookies.\\n- Filtering: you can filter the requests by type (XHR, JS, CSS, Img, Media, Font, Doc, WS) or by text in the URL.\\n- The analysis of performance: the Timing tab shows how long the DNS resolution, the establishment of the connection, the TLS handshake, the sending of the request and the receipt of the response took.\\n- The emulation of conditions: you can simulate a slow connection (Throttling) or switch the cache off (Disable cache).\\nAn example of use\\nSuppose you are developing an application in React that loads data through an API. You want to check that the request is being sent correctly and that the response arrives without errors. Open DevTools (F12), go to the Network tab, refresh the page or carry out the action that raises the request. You will see the request to your API. Click on it:\\n\\nIn Network you will see the status of the response (200 OK, for example), the headers (Content-Type, Authorization), the body of the response (JSON with the data) and the execution time. If the status is 404 or 500, you will notice it at once and be able to correct it.\\nConclusion\\nThe Network tab is the main tool for debugging the network interaction of web applications. It helps you find problems with the loading of resources quickly, analyse performance and check the correctness of API requests. Use it at the development and testing stage in order to ensure the stable working of an application.","// An example of code that makes a request\\nfetch('https://api.example.com/users')\\n  .then(response => response.json())\\n  .then(data => console.log(data))\\n  .catch(error => console.error('Error:', error));"],[895,"A HEAD request is similar to GET, but the server does not return the body of the response. It is used to check the existence of a resource, to obtain metadata (the headers) or to check the time of the last change without loading the content. That saves traffic and speeds the work up.","What is a HEAD request?\\nHEAD is an HTTP method that works identically to GET, but the server does not include the body of the response. In the response only the headers arrive. That lets the client obtain meta-information about a resource without downloading its content.\\nWhere and why is it applied?\\n\\n- Checking the existence of a URL (whether a link is broken, for example).\\n- Obtaining the Content-Type, Content-Length and Last-Modified before loading.\\n- Checking the cache: if the headers have not changed, the cached version can be used.\\n- The monitoring and testing of an API without the load of transferring data.\\nAn example of use\\n\\nIn this example we do not receive the body of the response, only the headers. That is fast and light.\\nConclusion\\nA HEAD request is useful for optimising work with resources, when you only have to check their availability or their metadata. Apply it to reduce traffic and speed up client applications.","fetch('https://api.example.com/data', { method: 'HEAD' })\\n  .then(response => {\\n    console.log('Status:', response.status);\\n    console.log('Content-Type:', response.headers.get('Content-Type'));\\n    console.log('Content-Length:', response.headers.get('Content-Length'));\\n  });"],[896,"Logging levels determine the importance of an event. Usually the following are used: TRACE (detailed debugging), DEBUG (debugging), INFO (information), WARN (a warning), ERROR (an error), FATAL (a critical error). The higher the level, the more serious the event. Filtering by level lets you manage the volume of the logs.","What are logging levels?\\nLogging levels are a mechanism for classifying the messages an application writes in the course of its work. They help developers and administrators assess the state of the system quickly by separating important events from insignificant ones. Every level has its own priority, and usually the logs are written only from a particular threshold upwards.\\nThe standard levels\\nThe most widespread hierarchy (from the least to the most critical):\\n\\n- TRACE — the smallest details of the execution; used rarely, only for deep debugging.\\n- DEBUG — debugging information; it helps a developer understand the flow of the execution.\\n- INFO — informational messages about the normal working of the application (the start, the finish, the key actions).\\n- WARN — warnings of potential problems that do not break the working but require attention.\\n- ERROR — errors that break the functionality, but the application can continue working.\\n- FATAL — critical errors, after which the application cannot continue working.\\nAn example of use in code (Node.js with Winston)\\n\\nIn the example the logging level has been set to 'info', so the debug message will be ignored.\\nWhere it is applied\\nLogging levels are used in every serious application and library: from web servers to mobile applications. They let you configure the volume of the information printed flexibly depending on the environment (development, testing, production).\\nConclusion: The use of logging levels is an industry standard for monitoring and debugging. The correct configuration of the levels helps you find problems quickly without overloading the system with excessive data.","const winston = require('winston');\\nconst logger = winston.createLogger({\\n  level: 'info', // log only info and above\\n  transports: [new winston.transports.Console()]\\n});\\n\\nlogger.debug('This will not be written');\\nlogger.info('The server has started on the port 3000');\\nlogger.warn('An obsolete method is being used');\\nlogger.error('Failed to connect to the database');"],[897,"A tuple is hashable only if all of its elements are hashable. If a tuple contains mutable objects such as a list or a dictionary, it cannot be hashed. That is connected with the fact that a hash function requires the immutability of an object in order to ensure consistency.","The hashability of tuples in Python\\nA tuple in Python is an immutable data type, but that does not guarantee its hashability. The hashability of a tuple depends on the hashability of all of its elements. If at least one element of the tuple is mutable (a list, a dictionary or a set, for example), then the tuple cannot be hashed.\\nExamples\\n\\nWhy does that matter?\\nHashability is necessary for using objects as the keys of dictionaries or as the elements of sets. If a tuple contains mutable elements, its hash can change, which would break the consistency of the data structures.\\nConclusion\\nA tuple is hashable only when all of its elements are hashable. That has to be taken into account when designing data structures, especially when using tuples as keys or in sets.","# A hashable tuple\\nhashable_tuple = (1, 2, 3)\\nprint(hash(hashable_tuple))  # Will print a number\\n\\n# A non-hashable tuple\\nnon_hashable_tuple = (1, [2, 3], 4)\\n# print(hash(non_hashable_tuple))  # TypeError: unhashable type: 'list'\\n\\n# A tuple with a tuple\\nnested_tuple = (1, (2, 3))\\nprint(hash(nested_tuple))  # Hashable, since the inner tuple is also immutable"],[898,"Multiprocessing is worth using for tasks that use the processor intensively (CPU-bound), when the limitations of the GIL in Python have to be bypassed. It creates separate processes with their own memory, which lets you make use of several cores of the processor. For I/O-bound tasks, multithreading or asynchrony suits better.","When to use multiprocessing\\nMultiprocessing in Python is applied for the parallel execution of tasks that require intensive computations on the processor (CPU-bound). The main reason is the Global Interpreter Lock (GIL), which does not let several threads of one process carry out Python code at the same time. Multiprocessing bypasses that limitation by creating separate processes, each with its own GIL.\\nExamples of use\\n\\n- The processing of images or video\\n- Numerical calculations and simulations\\n- The parsing of large volumes of data\\n- Machine learning (the training of models)\\nAn example of code\\n\\nWhen it is not worth using\\nFor I/O-bound tasks (work with the network, files, databases) multiprocessing is superfluous — threads or asynchrony suit better there. Multiprocessing also consumes more memory and resources for the creation of the processes.\\nConclusion: Multiprocessing is the optimal choice for CPU-bound tasks in Python, when the computations have to be speeded up through parallel execution on several cores of the processor.","import multiprocessing\\n\\ndef compute_square(n):\\n    return n * n\\n\\nif __name__ == '__main__':\\n    numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]\\n    with multiprocessing.Pool() as pool:\\n        results = pool.map(compute_square, numbers)\\n    print(results)  # [1, 4, 9, 16, 25]"],[899,"If two nested loops go over different arrays, then the overall complexity is equal to O(n * m), where n is the length of the first array and m that of the second. That is not quadratic complexity O(n²), since the arrays are different. Such an approach is often met when searching for intersections or comparing the elements of two collections.","The time complexity of two nested loops over different arrays\\nWhen we have two nested loops, each of which goes over its own array, the overall time complexity is computed as the product of the lengths of those arrays. If the first array has the size n and the second m, then the complexity will be O(n * m). It matters to distinguish that from the case where both loops go over one and the same array — then the complexity would be O(n²).\\nAn example in JavaScript\\n\\nIn this example 6 iterations are carried out, which corresponds to O(3 * 2). If the arrays had been of the same size, the complexity would have become O(n²), but here it depends linearly on both sizes.\\nWhere it is applied\\n\\n- The search for the intersections of two lists without the use of hash tables.\\n- The comparison of every element of one array with every element of another.\\n- The generation of the Cartesian product of two sets.\\nConclusion: Use O(n * m) to assess performance when nested loops work with different arrays. That is not quadratic complexity, but it can still be ineffective with large sizes of data — in such cases it is worth considering optimisation through hash tables or sorting.","const arr1 = [1, 2, 3]; // n = 3\\nconst arr2 = ['a', 'b']; // m = 2\\n\\nfor (let i = 0; i < arr1.length; i++) {\\n  for (let j = 0; j < arr2.length; j++) {\\n    console.log(arr1[i], arr2[j]);\\n  }\\n}\\n// Output: 1 a, 1 b, 2 a, 2 b, 3 a, 3 b — 6 operations in all (3 * 2)"],[900,"To stop the bubbling of an event in JavaScript, the \`event.stopPropagation()\` method is used. That prevents the further \\"bubbling\\" of the event to the parent elements, thereby restricting its handling to the current element only. If you have nested buttons, for example, that will help you avoid the unnecessary firing of the handlers on the parent elements.","The bubbling of an event is the process by which an event, having first fired on the target element, rises up the DOM tree, calling the event handlers on every parent element. That can be convenient, but it sometimes causes problems, when a single event starts several handlers at different levels of the DOM, causing undesirable behaviour.\\nThe \`event.stopPropagation()\` method lets you stop the further bubbling of an event after it has been handled on the current element. If you have nested elements with event handlers, for example, you may want to stop the event at a specific element, so that it does not fire on the parent elements.\\nAn example of the use of \`stopPropagation()\`:\\n\\nIn this example the click event is stopped at the child element, and it does not reach the parent element. That is useful in situations where you want to restrict the handling of events to a specific element, preventing undesirable reactions at the higher levels of the DOM.","document.querySelector('.child').addEventListener('click', function(event) {\\n  event.stopPropagation();    \\n  console.log('The event has been handled on the child element.'); \\n}); \\ndocument.querySelector('.parent').addEventListener('click', function() {\\n  console.log('This will not fire, since the bubbling has been stopped.'); \\n});"]]`);export{e as default};